**What changed:** Angular 22.0.0 added a compile-time check that throws when two `input()`, `output()` or `model()` declarations resolve to the same template-visible input or output name — including via aliases. Previously such duplicate bindings were silently allowed (last-one-wins / ambiguous runtime behaviour); now the build fails. Commit message example: `data = model(); dataChange = output(); // throws because model already emits on the `dataChange` output` and `userSomething = input({alias 'user'}); user = input(); // throws because userSomething already binds to the `user` input` (). Listed under "Breaking Changes → compiler" in the 22.0.0 release notes: "The compiler will throw when there a when inputs, outputs or model are binding to the same input/outputs." (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/22.0.x/CHANGELOG.md). Implemented by commit `03db2aefaa88bc73b6af6ed1c9c722b65079ab3b`, "fix(compiler): throw on duplicate input/outputs" (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/03db2ae).

**Affects:** Components/directives in the 22.0.0 build that declare two signal inputs, outputs, or `model()`s (directly, aliased, or via `model()`'s implicit `<name>Change` output) that map to the same input or output name.

**Action:** required: re-verify that no directive declares duplicate input/output names — remove or rename the offending `input()`/`output()`/`model()` declaration or change its alias so each input/output name is bound by exactly one declaration; fix all newly reported compile errors before the build succeeds.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// throws in v22: model already emits on the `dataChange` output
data = model();
dataChange = output();

// throws in v22: `userSomething` already binds to the `user` input
userSomething = input({alias: 'user'});
user = input();
```

**What changed:** Angular 22.0.0 raised its supported TypeScript range: `@angular/compiler-cli@22.0.0` now declares `peerDependencies.typescript: ">=6.0 <6.1"` (built/tested against `typescript: 6.0.3` in its devDependencies), so TypeScript versions older than 6.0 are no longer supported (npm registry metadata for `@angular/compiler-cli@22.0.0`, https://registry.npmjs.org/@angular/compiler-cli/22.0.0; changelog entry https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/main/CHANGELOG.md). The same 22.0.0 release also requires Node `^22.22.3 || ^24.15.0 || >=26.0.0` and pairs with `zone.js: ~0.15.0 || ~0.16.0` and `rxjs: ^6.5.3 || ^7.4.0` (https://registry.npmjs.org/@angular/core/22.0.0).

**Affects:** Applications whose `devDependencies.typescript` is pinned to a release older than 6.0 (e.g. 5.x), including `.d.ts`/type-checking behaviour of `ng build`, `ng test` and `ngc`; also projects with an explicit `typescript` pin in `package.json` or a monorepo-level TypeScript override.

**Action:** required: update the `typescript` devDependency to a 6.0.x release satisfying `>=6.0 <6.1`, then re-verify that `ng build` and `ng test` type-checking/reporting still passes and that unrelated TS 6.0 diagnostics in application code are addressed.

**Before/after:**
```json
{
  "devDependencies": {
    "typescript": "6.0.3"
  },
  "peerDependencies": {
    "typescript": ">=6.0 <6.1",
    "@angular/compiler": "22.0.0"
  }
}
```
(source: `@angular/compiler-cli@22.0.0` package metadata, https://registry.npmjs.org/@angular/compiler-cli/22.0.0)

**What changed:** The default change detection strategy for components with an undefined `changeDetection` property has changed from `Default` to `OnPush` in Angular v22. To retain the previous behavior, applications must explicitly specify `changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default` (note: the provided `Summary` incorrectly names the enum value `Eager`, which does not appear in the fetched Angular commit or changelog). The JIT compiler facade in v22 includes a fix that defaults components compiled with pre-v22 versions in partial compilation mode to `Default` change detection when running in JIT environments like Storybook or Jest, whereas v22+ components default to `OnPush` in both JIT and AOT (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3d104c0abbe4372f38ff97b684b857fb55aef297).

**Affects:** All applications using components where the `changeDetection` property is not explicitly defined in the `@Component` decorator metadata.

**Action:** required: Explicitly set `changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default` in the `@Component` decorator for all components that currently rely on the previous default behavior. Re-verify UI state updates and rendering lifecycle for all components.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Angular 21.2 and prior)
@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  template: '...'
})
export class ExampleComponent { }

// After (Required in Angular 22 to preserve behavior)
@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  template: '...',
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default
})
export class ExampleComponent { }
```

**What changed:** In Angular v22, `ComponentFactoryResolver` and `ComponentFactory` are no longer available. Instead of resolving a `ComponentFactory`, the component class (or `Type`) is passed directly to APIs that previously required a factory, such as `ViewContainerRef.createComponent` or the standalone `createComponent` function. The `ViewContainerRef` API in v22 includes a `createComponent` method that accepts a "Component Type" and an optional configuration object (`index`, `injector`, `ngModuleRef`, `environmentInjector`, `projectableNodes`, `directives`, `bindings`, `onError`), eliminating the need for factory resolution. `createComponent` is also listed as a top-level export in `@angular/core`.

**Affects:** All applications that previously used `ComponentFactoryResolver` to resolve `ComponentFactory` instances or passed factories to `ViewContainerRef.createComponent`.

**Action:** required: Remove imports of `ComponentFactoryResolver` and `ComponentFactory`. Update calls to `ViewContainerRef.createComponent` to pass the component class directly instead of a factory. Update usages of the standalone `createComponent` function to pass the component class. Ensure component providers are injected via `injector` or `environmentInjector` options in `ViewContainerRef.createComponent` if the component relies on non-root injectors previously provided by the factory context.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Angular 21 and earlier)
import { ComponentFactoryResolver, ComponentRef } from '@angular/core';
import { DynamicComponent } from './dynamic.component';

constructor(private resolver: ComponentFactoryResolver) {}
const factory = this.resolver.resolveComponentFactory(DynamicComponent);
const componentRef = this.viewContainerRef.createComponent(factory);

// After (Angular 22)
// Constructor injection of ComponentFactoryResolver is removed
const componentRef = this.viewContainerRef.createComponent(DynamicComponent, {
  injector: this.injector
});
```

**What changed:** Under the `@angular/platform-browser` "Breaking Changes" section, Angular v22 lists: "This removes styles when they appear to no longer be used by an associated `host`. However other DOM on the page may still be affected by those styles if not leveraging `ViewEncapsulation.Emulated` or if those styles are used by elements outside of Angular, potentially causing other DOM to appear unstyled." (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/22.0.x/CHANGELOG.md). Styles are removed at component destroy, governed by the DI token `REMOVE_STYLES_ON_COMPONENT_DESTROY` in `@angular/platform-browser`, "A DI token that indicates whether styles of destroyed components should be removed from DOM. By default, the value is set to `true`." (https://angular.dev/api/platform-browser/REMOVE_STYLES_ON_COMPONENT_DESTROY).

**Affects:** Applications whose component styles are relied upon by DOM not owned by an associated `host` — i.e. DOM that does not use `ViewEncapsulation.Emulated` (e.g. `ViewEncapsulation.None`/`ShadowDom`) or elements styled by Angular components that live outside Angular — so that removing styles on component destroy can leave that other DOM unstyled.

**Action:** required: re-verify visual/styling behaviour after components are destroyed; if styles must persist beyond a destroyed component's `host`, re-verify or set the `REMOVE_STYLES_ON_COMPONENT_DESTROY` DI token from `@angular/platform-browser` to change whether destroyed-component styles are removed (its default is `true`). The changelog gives no concrete code/config migration steps.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// DI token controlling this behaviour (default true = remove styles on destroy)
// REMOVE_STYLES_ON_COMPONENT_DESTROY from @angular/platform-browser
// "indicates whether styles of destroyed components should be removed from DOM. By default, the value is set to true."
```

**What changed:** The template type-checking compiler (TCB) updated safe navigation expression handling to allow correct narrowing of nullables. Previously, expressions like `foo?.bar.baz` in templates triggered "Object is possibly 'undefined'" errors even when the short-circuiting behavior made the access safe. With this change, the compiler correctly narrows types, but this can trigger new `nullishCoalescingNotNullable` and `optionalChainNotNullable` diagnostics in existing projects that relied on the previous incorrect typing or suppressed these errors. (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/47fcbc470462192c4f9e273d8dce8b353d5baaa2)

**Affects:** Applications using `strictTemplates` (which is default `true` in v22 configs) and `strictNullChecks` in `tsconfig.json` that use optional chaining (`?.`) or nullish coalescing (`??`) in templates, specifically where the new stricter type narrowing reveals potential type issues previously hidden.

**Action:** required: Re-verify template compilation for new `nullishCoalescingNotNullable` and `optionalChainNotNullable` errors. To temporarily suppress these errors without fixing them immediately, disable the diagnostics in `tsconfig.json`. To revert to the previous type-checking behavior for safe navigation, set `strictSafeNavigationTypes: false` in `angularCompilerOptions` in `tsconfig.json`.

**Before/after:**
```json
{
  "angularCompilerOptions": {
    "strictTemplates": true,
    "strictSafeNavigationTypes": false
  }
}
```

**What changed:** The Angular compiler's internal code generator, `AbstractEmitterVisitor` in `packages/compiler/src/output/abstract_emitter.ts`, previously emitted dynamic imports as `import(${ast.url})`, i.e. a string `url` was printed without quotes/escaping, producing invalid JavaScript. Fixed in commit `96be4f429ba316c75d2d4a39ececcc529ec10943` (`fix(compiler): abstract emitter producing incorrect code for dynamic imports`): `visitDynamicImportExpr` now prints `import(`, then, when `ast.url` is a `string`, emits it via the string/identifier escaping helper (`escapeStringOrIdentifier(ast.url, true)` on `main`, `escapeIdentifier(ast.url, true)` at fix time), and otherwise visits `ast.url` as an expression, then prints `)` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/96be4f429ba316c75d2d4a39ececcc529ec10943.patch, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/main/packages/compiler/src/output/abstract_emitter.ts). The same commit also changed line-breaking around function/arrow-function bodies (`ctx.println` → `ctx.print` plus `ctx.println(ast)` for `function (…) {`, `=> {`, and closing `}`) in `visitFunctionExpr`, `visitArrowFunctionExpr` and `visitDeclareFunctionStmt`, affecting only whitespace/layout of generated output.

**Affects:** Only the Angular-internal output emitter used when the compiler/`@angular/compiler` generates code containing `o.DynamicImportExpr` (e.g. lazy/deep imports in emitted output, including server/rendering output). Ordinary application source that writes `import('./x')` is unaffected, so typical applications see no change other than possibly regenerated/refreshed build caches with correctly quoted URLs.

**Action:** none — this is an internal compiler bug fix; no application code, configuration, or dependency change is required. Applications that previously post-processed or patched around unquoted dynamic import URLs in Angular-generated output should remove those workarounds and re-verify generated bundles.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before
visitDynamicImportExpr(ast: o.DynamicImportExpr, ctx: EmitterVisitorContext): void {
  this.printLeadingComments(ast, ctx);
  ctx.print(ast, `import(${ast.url})`);
}

// after
visitDynamicImportExpr(ast: o.DynamicImportExpr, ctx: EmitterVisitorContext): void {
  this.printLeadingComments(ast, ctx);
  ctx.print(ast, `import(`);
  if (typeof ast.url === 'string') {
    ctx.print(ast, this.escapeStringOrIdentifier(ast.url, true));
  } else {
    ast.url.visitExpression(this, ctx);
  }
  ctx.print(ast, `)`);
}
```

**What changed:** The Angular AOT compiler's output emitter no longer escapes `$` when emitting string literals. In `packages/compiler/src/output/abstract_emitter.ts`, the regex used by `escapeIdentifier` changed from `const SINGLE_QUOTE_ESCAPE_STRING_RE = /'|\\|\n|\r|\$/g;` to `const SINGLE_QUOTE_ESCAPE_STRING_RE = /'|\\|\n|\r/g;`, so `$` in an emitted single-quoted string literal is written as `$` instead of `\$` (the file also contains `LEGAL_IDENTIFIER_RE = /^[$A-Z_][0-9A-Z_$]*$/i`, unchanged) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/2c5aabb9daf5da3ad539381ef1e430c77583e3bf). Commit message: "Removes the escape for `$` in literal expressions. I don't think this is required with our output." Compliance tests were added (`packages/compiler-cli/test/compliance/test_cases/r3_compiler_compliance/components_and_directives/value_composition/dollar_escape.ts`/`.js`, case "should support dollar escape in template") covering a template like `` `\${{price}}` `` compiled to `$r3$.ɵɵtextInterpolate1("$", ctx.price)`. Both `\$\{...\}` and `\$` are valid JS string escapes producing the same runtime string, so emitted behavior is unchanged.

**Affects:** Only the text of compiler-generated output (e.g. `ɵcmp` template strings and other string literals containing `$`), such as AOT-published libraries / partial-compilation output (`ɵɵngDeclareComponent`, `$localize`, `$r3$` references). Application source code and runtime behaviour are not affected.

**Action:** none — re-verify only in the unusual case of tooling or tests that string-match on Angular's emitted output containing `\$`, since that sequence is no longer produced.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/compiler/src/output/abstract_emitter.ts
-const SINGLE_QUOTE_ESCAPE_STRING_RE = /'|\\|\n|\r|\$/g;
+const SINGLE_QUOTE_ESCAPE_STRING_RE = /'|\\|\n|\r/g;
```

**What changed:** In Angular 22.0.0 the compiler's ShadowCss shim (`packages/compiler/src/shadow_css.ts`) now requires `:host-context` to be followed by parentheses containing at least one non-whitespace argument before it is treated as Angular's `:host-context`. `_colonHostContextRe` changed from `/:host-context/gim` to `/:host-context(?=\(\s*[^)\s])/gim`, `_colonHostPattern` changed from `_polyfillHostContext + _parenSuffix + '?([^{]*)'` to `_polyfillHostContext + _parenSuffix + '([^{]*)'` (parens no longer optional), and `_colonHostRe` changed from `/:host/gim` to `/:host(?!\-context)/gim` so `:host` no longer matches inside `:host-context` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/c7aef8ec5dd12b5b1d4c703a61bd1dd43f998e18.patch). A bare `:host-context` (no parens) or `:host-context()` is now ignored by the shim and left in the output as a literal CSS pseudo-class instead of being expanded to the host scope. Updated tests in `host_and_host_context_spec.ts` show the new output.

**Affects:** Components whose CSS uses `:host-context` without parentheses (e.g. `:host-context .inner`) or with empty parentheses (`:host-context()`), in `ViewEncapsulation.Emulated` styling. `:host-context(.foo)` usage is unchanged.

**Action:** required: audit component styles for paren-less or empty-paren `:host-context`; replace such selectors with an explicit form (e.g. `:host-context(.some-class) .inner`), or re-verify that the previously host-scoped rules no longer apply, since they now emit the literal `:host-context` pseudo-class to the browser (which does not support it) and will stop matching.

**Before/after:**
```css
/* Input selector (shimmed by Angular, scope attr [contenta], host a-host) */
:host-context .inner {}
:host-context() .inner {}

/* Before (21.2): expanded to host scope */
[a-host] .inner[contenta] {}
[a-host] .inner[contenta] {}

/* After (22.0): left as literal pseudo-class */
[contenta]:host-context .inner[contenta] {}
[contenta]:host-context() .inner[contenta] {}
```

**What changed:** The Angular compiler previously generated invalid TypeScript when a directive/component `@Input` had a field name that is not a valid JS identifier and had coercion enabled (e.g. an input declared as `@Input({transform: (value: string) => value}) 'aria-label': string = '';`). Generated code used dot access for the coercion marker property, producing things like `typeof i0.Dir.ngAcceptInputType_aria-label` and unquoted object keys in type-check blocks and `.d.ts` output. The fix (compiler-cli `type_constructor.ts`, `transform.ts`, `declaration.ts` and `@angular/compiler` `typecheck/ops/inputs.ts`) adds an exported `isUnsafeObjectKey` helper (exported from `@angular/compiler`) and emits bracket-notation / quoted keys instead, e.g. `typeof i0.Dir["ngAcceptInputType_aria-label"]`, `{ "aria-label": typeof i0.Dir["ngAcceptInputType_aria-label"]; }`, and `static "ngAcceptInputType_aria-label": string;` in declaration files (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/b225a5d902f0ee1f6f68cde42266748cb1f2b1f8)

**Affects:** applications with `@Input` fields whose names need quoting in the source (e.g. `@Input('aria-label')` or `@Input() 'aria-label'`) combined with a coercion `transform` function, or code that parsed/inspected the compiler-generated type-check block or `.d.ts` output for such inputs.

**Action:** none — this is an internal compiler code-generation fix; upgrading applications require no code changes. Downstream tooling that scraped generated `.d.ts` output for coerced inputs with quoted names should re-verify it reads the new bracket-notation form.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Directive with coerced, quoted input name
@Directive({selector: '[dir]'})
export class Dir {
  @Input({transform: (value: string) => value}) 'aria-label': string = '';
}

// generated .d.ts before: static ngAcceptInputType_aria-label: string;  (invalid)
// generated .d.ts after:  static "ngAcceptInputType_aria-label": string;

// generated type-check block after:
var _t1 = null! as typeof i0.Dir["ngAcceptInputType_aria-label"];
```

**What changed:** The component style scoping logic in `ShadowCss._scopeAnimationRule` (`packages/compiler/src/shadow_css.ts`) changed its `animation`/`-webkit-animation` matching regular expression from `/((?:^|\s+|;)(?:-webkit-)?animation\s*:\s*),*([^;]+)/g` to `/((?:^|\s+|;)(?:-webkit-)?animation\s*:\s*)([^;]+)/g`. The removed `,*` previously absorbed and silently deleted any commas written immediately after `animation:` (e.g. `animation:, my-anim 1s` became `animation: my-anim 1s`); now those leading commas are preserved verbatim in the scoped/emulated-encapsulation CSS output. The legacy test "should handle (scope or not) animation definitions preceded by an erroneous comma" was deleted from `packages/compiler/test/shadow_css/keyframes_spec.ts`, so the old comma-stripping behaviour is no longer supported (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8a1533c9ad7c306e03d7c50676f87b56bade5bf6, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/770e505f784684891dbbeb2bd897d99f3c4c398a.patch)

**Affects:** Applications whose component CSS (with `encapsulation` using emulated shadow CSS scoping) contains an `animation:` or `-webkit-animation:` declaration that begins with a stray/erroneous comma, e.g. `animation:, my-anim 1s`.

**Action:** required: remove leading commas after `animation:` / `-webkit-animation:` in component styles so the declaration is valid CSS; re-verify that scoped `@keyframes` names in such rules still receive the `_ngcontent-..._` prefix.

**Before/after:**
```css
/* component CSS containing a stray leading comma */
.test {
  animation:, my-anim 1s,my-anim2 2s, my-anim3 3s,my-anim4 4s;
}
/* before: emitted as `animation: my-anim ...` (commas after `animation:` deleted)
   after:  the leading comma is preserved as written */
```

**What changed:** Removed support for `polyfill-next-selector`, `polyfill-unscoped-rule`, and `polyfill-rule` from `shadow_css.ts`. This includes removing their associated methods and regular expressions from the compiler, and deleting `polyfills_spec.ts` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/4c25a42e988e7a59d4d4dc3121cd77f7b344c048). These polyfill rules are no longer processed and no longer appear in the compiled output.

**Affects:** Applications with component CSS using the `polyfill-next-selector`, `polyfill-unscoped-rule`, or `polyfill-rule` at-rules.

**Action:** required: Remove `polyfill-next-selector`, `polyfill-unscoped-rule`, and `polyfill-rule` rules from component CSS.

**Before/after:**
```css
/* Old behavior (removed) */
polyfill-next-selector {
  content: ':host menu-item';
}
::content menu-item {
  color: red;
}

polyfill-rule {
  content: ':host menu-item';
  font-weight: bold;
}

@polyfill-unscoped-rule {
  content: 'menu-item';
  background: blue;
}
```

**What changed:** The ShadowCss compiler function `_convertColonHost` no longer splits a `:host(...)` selector on top-level commas and duplicates the rest of the rule for each selector. Previously, input like `:host(.x,.y) {}` was converted to `.x[a-host], .y[a-host] {}` by iterating over `this._splitOnTopLevelCommas(hostSelectors, true)` and producing one rule per part. Now, if the split yields more than one part, the original list is left as-is and emitted as `':host(' + hostSelectors + ')' + otherSelectors`, so subsequent arguments are ignored rather than split and duplicated (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1d3bf59f9ab11add91cc81fee6b7b302a168db85). The obsolete `host_and_host_context_spec.ts` cases ("should handle multiple tag selectors", "should handle multiple class selectors", "should handle multiple attribute selectors") were replaced by "should ignore :host with a selector list containing top-level commas" (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/1d3bf59f9ab11add91cc81fee6b7b302a168db85).

**Affects:** applications whose component styles use `:host(...)` with a comma-separated selector list (e.g. `:host(ul,li)`, `:host(.x,.y)`, `:host([a="b"],[c=d])`) in scoped/shadow CSS.

**Action:** required: re-verify the compiled scoped CSS output for components that use `:host(...)` with a selector list; the previous duplicated-selector output (`.x[a-host], .y[a-host] {}`) is replaced by an unpolyfilled passthrough form (`[contenta]:host(.a, .b) {}`), so any layout or selector specificity relying on the split rules must be checked.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before
expect(shim(':host(.x,.y) {}', 'contenta', 'a-host')).toEqualCss('.x[a-host], .y[a-host] {}');

// After
expect(shim(':host(.a, .b) {}', 'contenta', 'a-host')).toEqualCss(
  '[contenta]:host(.a, .b) {}',
);
```

**What changed:** The Angular compiler (`@angular/compiler` / AOT template compilation) now throws a compile-time error when two `input()`, `output()`, or `model()` declarations on a directive/component bind to the same public input or output name — either within a single directive or across a directive and the component it extends (03db2aefaa88bc73b6af6ed1c9c722b65079ab3b). Previously the later declaration silently overrode the earlier one; e.g. an explicit `output('valueChange')` overrode the implicit `valueChange` output of `model('value')`, so the model's changes stopped being emitted (angular/angular#65844). Examples that now throw: `data = model(); dataChange = output();` (the model already emits on `dataChange`), and `userSomething = input({alias: 'user'}); user = input();` (`userSomething` already binds to the `user` input) (commit 03db2aefaa88bc73b6af6ed1c9c722b65079ab3b).

**Affects:** Components/directives using signal functions `input()`, `output()`, or `model()` where two members resolve to the same input/output public name, including via `alias` options or a `model`'s implicit `<name>Change` output colliding with an explicit `output`.

**Action:** required: remove or rename the duplicate member so only one declaration binds each input/output name (change the property name or the `alias`), or drop the redundant explicit `output` when a `model` already provides the `<name>Change` output. Re-verify that parents listening via `<name>Change` still receive updates.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Angular ≤21.2): compiled, but the output silently overrode the model's implicit output
data = model();
dataChange = output(); // overrides model's `dataChange`

// Now throws in Angular 22: `model` already emits on the `dataChange` output
// Also throws:
userSomething = input({alias: 'user'});
user = input(); // `userSomething` already binds to the `user` input
```
(https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/03db2aefaa88bc73b6af6ed1c9c722b65079ab3b, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/65844)

I have enough from the two commit patches.

**What changed:** Previously, when a `@for` loop in a template omitted the required `track` expression, the Angular compiler produced **no** `ForLoopBlock` AST node at all — only the parse error `@for loop must have a "track" expression` — so nothing inside the block got type-checked or language-service support. Now the AST is produced anyway, with `ForLoopBlock.trackBy` and `ForLoopBlock.trackKeywordSpan` becoming nullable: `public trackBy: ASTWithSource | null` and `public trackKeywordSpan: ParseSourceSpan | null` in `packages/compiler/src/render3/r3_ast.ts`, with the same nullability flowing to `TmplAstForLoopBlock` consumers (`packages/core/schematics/.../template_reference_visitor.ts`, `packages/language-service/src/quick_info_built_ins.ts`, `template_target.ts`). Content of a `track`-less `@for` body is now type checked, e.g. a template with `@for (item of items) { {{does_not_exist}} }` now reports both `Property 'does_not_exist' does not exist on type 'Main'.` and `@for loop must have a "track" expression` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ccb7d427e4f07506c14c50ce0cbe87c57930ebb5, ).

**Affects:** (a) applications whose templates contain an invalid `@for` block without `track` — new type-check errors from the block body can now surface and fail `ng build`/`ng test`; (b) code that reads `block.trackBy` or `block.trackKeywordSpan` on a `ForLoopBlock` / `TmplAstForLoopBlock` (custom schematics, transformers, language-service extensions), which must now handle `null`.

**Action:** required: add `track` to any `@for` loop that lacks one and re-verify template type-check diagnostics for previously hidden errors inside such loops; in code that visits the template AST, null-check `block.trackBy` / `block.trackKeywordSpan` before calling `.visit(...)`, `.ast` or passing them to `templateTypeChecker.getSymbolOfNode(...)`, i.e. use `block.trackBy?.visit(...)`, `forLoopNode.trackBy!.ast`, or `if (block.trackBy !== null) { ... }`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// ForLoopBlock AST field types (packages/compiler/src/render3/r3_ast.ts)
-    public trackBy: ASTWithSource,
-    public trackKeywordSpan: ParseSourceSpan,
+    public trackBy: ASTWithSource | null,
+    public trackKeywordSpan: ParseSourceSpan | null,

// visitor code
-    nodeOrNodes.trackBy.visit(this);
+    nodeOrNodes.trackBy?.visit(this);

// template that now yields extra diagnostics
@for (item of items) {
  {{does_not_exist}}
}
```

