```

**What changed:** In v22 the default `HttpBackend` used by `provideHttpClient()` switched from `HttpXhrBackend` to `FetchBackend`: `provideHttpClient` now always provides `FetchBackend` and its `HttpBackend` factory is `inject(FetchBackend)`, the internal `FETCH_BACKEND` token and `NG_DEFAULT_HTTP_BACKEND` were removed, and `withFetch()` is now a no-op passthrough feature (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3bc095d508653982a48b337afd51bfedbbde1f87). To preserve existing behaviour, a new automatic migration schematic `http-xhr-backend` (in `packages/core/schematics/migrations.json`, version `22.0.0`, factory `./bundles/http-xhr-backend.cjs#migrate`, class `XhrBackendMigration`) rewrites `provideHttpClient(...)` calls imported from `@angular/common/http`: it inserts `withXhr()` as the first feature argument and adds the `withXhr` import, or, when `withFetch()` was present, removes `withFetch()` instead (since fetch is now the default). It leaves calls alone when `withXhr()` is already present (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3bc095d508653982a48b337afd51bfedbbde1f87). `withXhr()` "Configures the current HttpClient instance to make requests using the Xhr API" and must not be used in SSR, where XHR support is deprecated and slated for removal in Angular 23 (https://angular.dev/api/common/http/withXhr). By default HttpClient now uses the fetch API (https://angular.dev/api/common/http/provideHttpClient).

**Affects:** applications calling `provideHttpClient()` from `@angular/common/http` that relied on the previous XHR default (including those that passed `withFetch()`).

**Action:** required: run `ng update` so the automatic `http-xhr-backend` schematic rewrites the calls; then verify the generated result — applications relying on XHR-specific behaviour (e.g. upload progress reporting, which the Xhr API supports) should keep `withXhr()`, while SSR apps should drop the schematic's `withXhr()` in favour of the fetch default, since XHR on the server is deprecated for removal in Angular 23 (https://angular.dev/api/common/http/withXhr).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before (21.x, XHR default) — schematic adds withXhr()
provideHttpClient(withInterceptorsFromDi(), withXsrfConfiguration({}));
// after (22.0.0)
provideHttpClient(withXhr(), withInterceptorsFromDi(), withXsrfConfiguration({}));

// before — schematic removes withFetch() (fetch is now the default)
provideHttpClient(withFetch(), withInterceptorsFromDi());
// after
provideHttpClient(withInterceptorsFromDi());
```
(from the migration's spec, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3bc095d508653982a48b337afd51bfedbbde1f87)

**What changed:** Angular 22 adds an opt-in SSR transfer cache for `resource`/`rxResource`. A new optional `id` property was added to `BaseResourceOptions` in `@angular/core`: "Identifier used to cache the resource data in the `TransferState` during server-side rendering and to retrieve it on the client side. This value needs to be identical for both the client and server." (https://angular.dev/api/core/BaseResourceOptions). In the implementation, `ResourceImpl` now takes a `transferCacheKey: StateKey<T> | undefined` and, when the cache is active and a key is present, resolves the resource synchronously from `TransferState` (`transferState.hasKey(cacheKey)` / `transferState.get(cacheKey, defaultValue)`) instead of running the loader; on the server (`ngServerMode`) a resolved value is written back via `transferState.set(transferCacheKey, result.value)` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/5a7c1e62dc2a4fa199b85150eca66914c107a6f4). Cache activation uses a new private token `ɵCACHE_ACTIVE` (`packages/core/src/hydration/cache.ts`, `InjectionToken<{isActive: boolean}>`), which `provideClientHydration()` in `@angular/platform-browser` now provides as `{isActive: true}` and flips to `false` in an `APP_BOOTSTRAP_LISTENER` once `appRef.whenStable()` resolves; writes only happen on the server (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/5a7c1e62dc2a4fa199b85150eca66914c107a6f4).

**Affects:** Applications using `resource()` / `rxResource()` (including `httpResource`) with SSR. Only affected at runtime if the new `id` option is used; no existing behaviour changes for resources without `id`.

**Action:** none (purely additive opt-in feature). Optionally, to opt in, add `id` to the resource options so the loader result is served from `TransferState` on the client; the value must be identical on server and client.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before: loader always runs on the client after hydration
const user = resource({loader: async () => fetchUser()});

// After (Angular 22): server result is transferred via TransferState
const user = resource({
  loader: async () => fetchUser(),
  id: 'user-resource', // must match on server and client
});
```

**What changed:** In Angular v22, `OnPush` becomes the default change detection strategy for all components ((https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cb4cb77053a817fe800af6395783720761e29ada)). To maintain the existing behavior for apps that relied on the previous default (Eager), a new automatic migration is introduced ((https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cb4cb77053a817fe800af6395783720761e29ada)). This migration identifies components and directives that do not explicitly define a `changeDetection` strategy and adds `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager` to their metadata ((https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cb4cb77053a817fe800af6395783720761e29ada)). This ensures that upgrading to v22 does not unintentionally switch existing components to `OnPush` behavior ((https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cb4cb77053a817fe800af6395783720761e29ada)).

**Affects:** Applications containing components or directives that do not explicitly specify a `changeDetection` strategy in their `@Component` or `@Directive` metadata.

**Action:** required: Run the Angular update migrations via `ng update @angular/core@22.0.0` to automatically apply `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager` to components lacking an explicit strategy setting.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
import { Component, ChangeDetectionStrategy } from '@angular/core';

// Before v22 (implied default was Eager)
@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  template: `<h1>Hello</h1>`
})
export class ExampleComponent { }

// After v22 with migration applied (default is OnPush, so Eager is added explicitly)
@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  template: `<h1>Hello</h1>`,
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager
})
export class ExampleComponent { }
```

