});
```

**What changed:** Angular 22.0 adds a new experimental API `debounced` exported from `@angular/core`: `export function debounced<T>(source: () => T, wait: NoInfer<number | ((value: T, lastValue: ResourceSnapshot<T>) => Promise<void> | void)>, options?: NoInfer<DebouncedOptions<T>>): Resource<T>`, along with `export interface DebouncedOptions<T> { equal?: ValueEqualityFn<T>; injector?: Injector; }` (marked `@experimental 22.0`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/b918beda323eefef17bf1de03fde3d402a3d4af0). It returns a `Resource` whose `value()` holds the debounced signal value and whose `status()` is `resolved` (settled), `loading` (a value is pending debounce; the previous value is retained), or `error` (source signal threw). `wait` is either a millisecond number (implemented via `setTimeout`) or a custom function returning a promise (async) or `void` (synchronous, goes straight to `resolved`). Rapid updates restart the debounce; equal values (default `Object.is`, overridable via `equal`) do not. It requires an injection context unless an `injector` is passed, and cleans up via `DestroyRef.onDestroy`. The same commit also adds `RuntimeErrorCode.INVALID_RESOURCE_CREATION_IN_PARAMS = 992` with message "Cannot create a resource inside the `params` of another resource" — creating a `debounced` (or `resource`) inside another resource's `params` now throws this `RuntimeError` instead of failing silently.

**Affects:** Opt-in only — nothing changes for existing apps unless they call the new `debounced` API. Separately, code that creates a resource inside another resource's `params` function now throws error code 992.

**Action:** none — this is an additive, purely opt-in capability; no code, config, or dependency changes are required to upgrade. Adopt `debounced(...)` from `@angular/core` only where signal debouncing is wanted, and note that misuse inside a resource `params` function raises `RuntimeError` code 992.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// New in 22.0 (from debounce_spec.ts) — opt-in usage:
import {signal, debounced} from '@angular/core';

const source = signal('initial');
const res = debounced(source, 10, {injector}); // returns Resource<string>

// source.set('updated') -> res.status() === 'loading', res.value() === 'initial'
// after 10ms             -> res.status() === 'resolved', res.value() === 'updated'

// custom wait + equality:
const res2 = debounced(source, (val, last) => myPromise(val), {
  injector,
  equal: (a, b) => a.id === b.id,
});
```

**What changed:** The `@switch` control-flow block's exhaustiveness check `@default never;` now accepts an optional expression argument: `@default never(<expression>);`. Previously the compiler always type-checked the `@switch` expression itself as `never`, which fails when the switched expression is only a discriminant of a union (e.g. `@switch (state.mode)` with `state!: {mode: 'hide'} | {mode: 'show'; menu: number};`), so exhaustiveness could not be used for values introduced by `@for`/`@if` or nested discriminant properties (fixes #67406). The new parameter is parsed into a new `expression: AST | null` field on `SwitchExhaustiveCheck` (`packages/compiler/src/render3/r3_ast.ts`), visited by `TemplateBinder.visitSwitchExhaustiveCheck` and `TemplateTargetVisitor.visitSwitchExhaustiveCheck`, and used by `TcbSwitchOp` in `packages/compiler-cli/src/ngtsc/typecheck/src/ops/switch_block.ts`, which emits e.g. `const tcbExhaustive_t1: never = ((((this).expr)).prop);` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8bc31a515ff6e8edda6ea5786a47ae5a788acd36)

**Affects:** Templates using the `@switch` block's `@default never;` exhaustiveness check, in particular where the switched expression is a nested/property discriminant of a union or a variable from `@for`/`@if`. Existing plain `@default never;` usage keeps its previous behaviour.

**Action:** none (opt-in syntax addition). Optionally replace plain `@default never;` with `@default never(<expr>);` where the switched value is a union discriminant so the check narrows correctly.

**Before/after:**
```angular-ts
@Component({
  template: `
    @switch (state.mode) {
      @case ('show') { {{ state.menu }}; }
      @case ('hide') {}
      @default never(state);
    }
  `,
})
export class App {
  state!: {mode: 'hide'} | {mode: 'show'; menu: number};
}
```

**What changed:** The `ResourceStreamingLoader` type in `@angular/core` was widened from `(param: ResourceLoaderParams<R>) => PromiseLike<Signal<ResourceStreamItem<T>>>` to `(param: ResourceLoaderParams<R>) => Signal<ResourceStreamItem<T>> | PromiseLike<Signal<ResourceStreamItem<T>>> | undefined`, so `resource({stream: ...})` loaders may return a `Signal` synchronously instead of only a promise (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/4e331062e8385e066102c3bbb8be439eabfdf8c9, confirmed in `packages/core/src/resource/api.ts`). `ResourceImpl` now calls the loader without `await`, detects a returned signal via `isSignal` and sets state immediately; promises are still awaited. Motivation: cached SSR values (e.g. via `TransferState`) must resolve synchronously, otherwise the resource sits in `"loading"` state and destroys the server-hydrated DOM during hydration. `rxResource` (`@angular/core/rxjs-interop`) also returns the stream `Signal` synchronously when the Observable emits synchronously (`if (hasResolved) { return stream; }`), otherwise the promise. Behavioural side effect visible in the updated specs: `rxResource`/`resource` streams are no longer evaluated eagerly — they run inside an effect, so a value/status only appears after a tick (`TestBed.tick()` / `appRef.whenStable()`); a synchronous error (e.g. `throwError(...)`, or an Observable completing with no value → `RESOURCE_COMPLETED_BEFORE_PRODUCING_VALUE` / `NG0991`) is reported in `error` status right after that tick.

**Affects:** Applications using `resource({stream: ...})` or `rxResource(...)`, especially under SSR with `TransferState` caching, and tests that asserted `rxResource` values without first triggering change detection.

**Action:** required: re-verify resource-backed SSR hydration (no `"loading"` flash of server-rendered DOM) and re-verify tests/code that read `res.value()` / `res.status()` right after creating a `resource`/`rxResource` — a tick is now needed since the stream is not evaluated eagerly. Optionally return a synchronous `Signal` from a `stream` loader to make cached values available immediately.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before (21.2)
export type ResourceStreamingLoader<T, R> = (
  param: ResourceLoaderParams<R>,
) => PromiseLike<Signal<ResourceStreamItem<T>>>;

// after (22.0)
export type ResourceStreamingLoader<T, R> = (
  param: ResourceLoaderParams<R>,
) => Signal<ResourceStreamItem<T>> | PromiseLike<Signal<ResourceStreamItem<T>>> | undefined;

// new synchronous form
const res = resource({
  stream: () => signal({value: 'done'}),
  injector: TestBed.inject(Injector),
});
TestBed.tick();
expect(res.status()).toBe('resolved');
```

**What changed:** Angular 22 replaces the previous runtime error `NG0309: Directives can only match an element once.` with de-duplication logic in the compiler and runtime for `hostDirectives` (Directive Composition API). Rules: (1) If a directive matches once in the template *and* one or more times as a host directive, the host directive matches are discarded and only the template match applies — a host directive match is treated as `Partial<YourDirective>`, a template match as the full `YourDirective`. (2) If a directive matches multiple times as a host directive, the input/output mappings from all instances are merged into a single one; if during merging an input/output is exposed under multiple names, both the compiler and the runtime produce an error (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9c55fcb3e65ffcde32d7ac438ea40a69ffc2b3b6). This addresses issue #57846, where reusing a shared host directive (e.g. an `appearance` directive used by both a button and a dropdown item directive) caused `NG0309` when both were listed in `hostDirectives` (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/57846).

**Affects:** Applications/libraries using `hostDirectives` where the same directive class can match an element more than once (via multiple host directive chains, or once in the template plus as a host directive), and code that previously relied on `NG0309` being thrown for duplicates.

**Action:** none for the common case — previously failing `NG0309` scenarios now compile and run. However, re-verify elements with duplicated host directive matches: confirm the intended input/output aliases still apply, since template matches now override host directive matches and duplicate host matches are merged. Also verify that no input/output of a shared directive is aliased under two different names in different host directive configurations, since that is now a compile-time and runtime error.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Angular 21): throwing NG0309 when a host directive was matched twice.
// After (Angular 22): de-duplicated - the input/output mappings are merged.
@Directive({
  selector: '[appearance]',
})
export class AppearanceDirective {}

@Directive({
  selector: '[button]',
  hostDirectives: [AppearanceDirective],
})
export class ButtonDirective {}

@Directive({
  selector: '[dropdownItem]',
  hostDirectives: [AppearanceDirective],
})
export class DropdownItemDirective {}

@Directive({
  selector: '[sidebarItem]',
  hostDirectives: [ButtonDirective, DropdownItemDirective],
})
export class SidebarItemDirective {}
```

**What changed:** Angular 22 exports an experimental WebMCP API from `@angular/core`: `declareWebMcpTool<const InputSchema extends JsonSchemaForInference>(tool: WebMcpToolDescriptor<InputSchema>, injector?: Injector): void`, plus the types `WebMcpToolDescriptor` (`name`, `description?`, `inputSchema`, `execute`), `WebMcpToolExecute`, and `WebMcpClient` (`signal: AbortSignal`) (commit ef1810197b, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ef1810197b679bfcbf21a139b930984302cbe77f). The tool is registered immediately via `navigator.modelContext.registerTool` and is unregistered automatically when the associated `Injector` is destroyed (uses `DestroyRef`); it is a no-op when `navigator.modelContext` is unavailable. JSON Schema types are deliberately not re-exported — users needing them add their own dependency on `@mcp-b/webmcp-types`. It throws `NG0203` when called outside an injection context without an explicit `injector` argument (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/ef1810197b679bfcbf21a139b930984302cbe77f/packages/core/src/webmcp/declare_tool.ts)

**Affects:** Nothing pre-existing; purely additive new capability. Only relevant to apps that adopt `declareWebMcpTool` (requires an injection context or an explicit `Injector`).

**Action:** none — no migration required; adopt the new API optionally. Note the API is marked `@experimental` and its types are intentionally not coupled to `@mcp-b/webmcp-types`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// new public API surface in @angular/core (goldens/public-api/core/index.api.md)
export function declareWebMcpTool<const InputSchema extends JsonSchemaForInference>(tool: WebMcpToolDescriptor<InputSchema>, injector?: Injector): void;
export interface WebMcpClient {
    signal: AbortSignal;
}
export interface WebMcpToolDescriptor<InputSchema extends JsonSchemaForInference> {
    description?: string;
    execute: WebMcpToolExecute<InputSchema>;
    inputSchema: InputSchema;
    name: string;
}
export type WebMcpToolExecute<InputSchema extends JsonSchemaForInference> = (args: InferArgsFromInputSchema<InputSchema>, client: WebMcpClient) => unknown;
```

**What changed:** Angular 22.0.0 introduces a new `@Service` decorator as an alternative to `@Injectable`. `@Service` defaults to `providedIn: 'root'`, supports only `inject`-function-based DI (no constructor-based injection), and accepts a simplified options object with only `autoProvided?: boolean` and `factory?: () => unknown` (no `useClass`/`useValue` etc.). Applying a constructor-based injectable parameter to an `@Service` class triggers new error code `SERVICE_CONSTRUCTOR_DI` (2028) ("Cannot apply more than one Angular decorator on an @Service class"). Applying another `@angular/core` decorator to an `@Service` class is rejected. The decorator is marked `@developerPreview 22.0`. The JIT metadata function is `ɵɵdefineService({token, autoProvided, factory})`, setting `providedIn: 'root'` unless `autoProvided === false` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8f3d0b9d97424e058eb7bce57d80833fb68dec4a, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8f3d0b9d97424e058eb7bce57d80833fb68dec4a.patch).

**Affects:** New applications choosing to define services with `@Service`; existing code using `@Injectable` is unaffected (it remains fully supported).

**Action:** none — this is an additive feature; migrating existing `@Injectable` classes to `@Service` is optional and requires verifying that services do not use constructor injection or complex provider types.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// New form introduced in 22.0.0 (optional alternative to @Injectable):
import {Service} from '@angular/core';

@Service()
export class PostService {
  private readonly httpClient = inject(HttpClient);
  private readonly authService = inject(AuthService);

  getUserPosts() {
    return this.httpClient.get('/api/posts/' + this.authService.userId);
  }
}

// Opt out of automatic 'root' provision:
@Service({autoProvided: false})
export class MyService {}

// Custom factory:
@Service({factory: () => new Alternate()})
export class MyService {}
```

**What changed:** In Angular v22 the default `changeDetection` strategy for `@Component` is now `OnPush`. Components whose `changeDetection` property is left undefined were previously checked eagerly (the old `Default` behavior); they are now `OnPush` by default (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/eae8f7e30b9f8bebdcdb535bd86260199c34274b). Per the v22 API docs, `ChangeDetectionStrategy.OnPush` carries the note "NOTE: OnPush is enabled by default."; `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager` means "the component is checked eagerly when the change detection traversal reaches it, rather than only checking under certain circumstances (e.g. `markForCheck`, a signal in the template changed, etc.)"; and `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Default` is now documented as "Use `Eager` instead. This value is equivalent to setting `Eager` and is due to be removed." (https://v22.angular.dev/api/core/ChangeDetectionStrategy)
**Affects:** Any `@Component` that does not explicitly set `changeDetection` (or that relied on the previous `Default`/eager default). Templates that mutate state via plain class fields, `EventEmitter`/promise/setTimeout callbacks, or async pipes without `signal`/`markForCheck` may stop re-rendering.
**Action:** required: for every component that must keep the previous behavior, add `changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager` to its `@Component` decorator and import `ChangeDetectionStrategy` from `@angular/core`; alternatively re-verify views that mutate state without signals or `ChangeDetectorRef.markForCheck()`. Prefer replacing `Default` with `Eager`, since `Default` is due to be removed.
**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before (v21): implicitly eager
@Component({selector: 'app-foo', template: '...'})

// after (v22): keep old behavior explicitly
@Component({
  selector: 'app-foo',
  template: '...',
  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager,
})
```

**What changed:** Angular now detects the actual root node of a bootstrapped component's host element and uses it as the style host instead of unconditionally using `document.head`. During bootstrap in `ComponentFactory`, `ComponentRef` / `ApplicationRef.bootstrap`, a new internal helper `getStyleHost(node, doc)` inspects `hostElement.getRootNode?.()`; if the node is connected to the `Document` it returns `doc.head`, if `getRootNode` is unavailable (e.g. Node.js) it falls back to `doc().head`, if the node is inside a `ShadowRoot` it returns that `ShadowRoot`, and for disconnected nodes it falls back to `doc().head`. The resolved host is then passed to `SharedStylesHost.addHost(styleHost)`, so component styles are written into the enclosing shadow root when an application is bootstrapped underneath one (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cdda51a3b2f48d5623acef0c6f54afb7af921b58). On destroy, styles are removed only when the style host is a `ShadowRoot` (assumed to contain a single Angular root component); styles placed in the main document are intentionally left in place, because `SharedStylesHost` does not reference-count hosts and calling `removeHost` would strip styles still needed by other components on the page (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cdda51a3b2f48d5623acef0c6f54afb7af921b58). This is a new capability, not a breaking API change.

**Affects:** Applications that bootstrap a root component inside an element attached to a `ShadowRoot` (micro-frontend / web-component embedding, `bootstrapApplication` or `ApplicationRef.bootstrap` against a host element within a shadow tree). Applications that keep their root component in the main document light DOM are unaffected.

**Action:** none — no code, configuration or dependency changes are required to gain the behaviour; re-verify style injection and SSR-hydration style deduplication for applications that bootstrap under shadow roots, since style removal on destroy is scoped to shadow-root hosts only (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/cdda51a3b2f48d5623acef0c6f54afb7af921b58).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/core/src/render3/component_ref.ts
const sharedStylesHost = rootViewInjector.get(SHARED_STYLES_HOST, null);
const styleHost = getStyleHost(
  hostElement,
  () => rootViewInjector.get(DOCUMENT, null) ?? getDocument(),
);
if (sharedStylesHost) sharedStylesHost.addHost(styleHost);

// Only shadow-root hosts are cleaned up on destroy (no reference counting in SharedStylesHost).
if (sharedStylesHost && shadowRootSupported && styleHost instanceof ShadowRoot) {
  storeLViewOnDestroy(rootLView, () => {
    sharedStylesHost.removeHost(styleHost);
  });
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 22 makes the `on idle` trigger of `@defer` customizable via DI. New public API in `@angular/core`: an `IdleService` interface with `requestOnIdle(callback: (deadline?: IdleDeadline) => void): number` and `cancelOnIdle(id: number): void`, plus a `provideIdleServiceWith(useExisting: AbstractType<IdleService> | InjectionToken<IdleService>): EnvironmentProviders` function that binds an app-provided implementation to the internal `IDLE_SERVICE` injection token (default implementation uses `requestIdleCallback` when available or `setTimeout` otherwise) ((https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/a5981b83a60577d9068d2429bcbed969edca581b)). Internally, `IdleScheduler` now injects `IDLE_SERVICE` and calls `idleService.requestOnIdle` / `idleService.cancelOnIdle` instead of its previously public-on-the-instance properties `requestIdleCallbackFn` and `cancelIdleCallbackFn`, which were removed from `IdleScheduler` ((same commit, diff of `packages/core/src/defer/idle_scheduler.ts`)). Callbacks still run inside `NgZone` via `ngZone.run`.

**Affects:** Only opt-in usage — no existing application behavior changes (default `RequestIdleCallbackService` preserves old behavior). Affected are tests/code that stubbed the removed `scheduler.requestIdleCallbackFn` / `cancelIdleCallbackFn` on `IdleScheduler` (previously used to fake `requestIdleCallback` behavior), or apps wanting custom idle scheduling.

**Action:** none — unless code monkey-patched `IdleScheduler.requestIdleCallbackFn`/`cancelIdleCallbackFn`; then re-verify that behavior and migrate to providing a class implementing `IdleService` via `provideIdleServiceWith(...)` (in `TestBed.configureTestingModule({providers: [...]})` or app config) or `{provide: IDLE_SERVICE, useValue: ...}`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (test stubbing of the removed IdleScheduler fields):
scheduler.requestIdleCallbackFn = jasmine
  .createSpy('requestIdleCallbackFn')
  .and.callFake((cb: any) => { /* ... */ });

// After (Angular 22): implement IdleService and provide it
@Injectable({providedIn: 'root'})
class CustomIdleService implements IdleService {
  requestOnIdle(callback: (deadline?: IdleDeadline) => void): number { /* ... */ }
  cancelOnIdle(id: number): void { /* ... */ }
}

TestBed.configureTestingModule({
  providers: [provideIdleServiceWith(CustomIdleService)],
});
```

**What changed:** The `on idle` deferred trigger in `@defer` blocks now accepts an optional timeout parameter. Previously, `@defer (on idle)` invoked `requestIdleCallback` without configuration options; now syntax like `@defer (on idle(500ms))` passes the timeout to the underlying `requestIdleCallback`. The `IdleDeferredTrigger` AST node in `packages/compiler/src/render3/r3_ast.ts` now includes a `timeout` property, and the generated instruction `$r3$.ɵɵdeferOnIdle(timeout)` (e.g., `$r3$.ɵɵdeferOnIdle(500)`) receives the integer value (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/98eb24cea0498382cc7cf7d7b85cd9ead5ad99ad). The `IdleService` interface method `requestOnIdle` now accepts an optional `options?: IdleRequestOptions` parameter, and the `IdleScheduler` batches callbacks into buckets based on their serialized options to ensure distinct timeouts do not interfere (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/main/packages/core/src/defer/idle_service.ts).

**Affects:** Applications using `@defer` blocks with the `idle` trigger, particularly those providing a custom `IdleService` implementation or relying on the default idle scheduling behavior for deferred content.

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// packages/compiler-cli/test/compliance/test_cases/r3_view_compiler_deferred/deferred_on_idle_with_timeout.ts

@Component({
    template: `
    @defer (on idle(500ms)) {
      {{message}}
    } @placeholder {
      <p>Placeholder</p>
    }
  `,
})
export class MyApp {
  message = 'hello';
}
```
```typescript
// Generated template instruction (before/after comparison via GOLDEN_PARTIAL)
// After:
$r3$.ɵɵdeferOnIdle(500);
```

**What changed:** `Testability.isStable()` previously returned `this._isZoneStable && !this._ngZone.hasPendingMacrotasks`; in v22 it additionally requires `!this.pendingTasksInternal.hasPendingTasks` when pending-task-based stability is enabled (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9f479ae9641a5c928f8eeab9c7846245002b3eff). A new internal token `ɵUSE_PENDING_TASKS` (default `typeof Zone === 'undefined'`) controls this, and `Testability` now also subscribes to `PendingTasksInternal.hasPendingTasksObservable` to fire `whenStable` callbacks. Since zone stability and `Router`/`HttpClient` already contribute to `PendingTasks`, this makes `Testability` (and Protractor `whenStable`, ComponentHarness-based integration tests) correct in zoneless apps (fixes https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/68180). Public API change: `provideProtractorTestingSupport()` became `provideProtractorTestingSupport(options?: {usePendingTasksForStability?: boolean})`, which provides `ɵUSE_PENDING_TASKS` when the option is set (ignored otherwise with ZoneJS present).

**Affects:** zoneless applications (no `Zone` global) whose integration/E2E tests rely on `Testability.whenStable`, `browser.waitForAngular()`, Protractor `whenStable`, or ComponentHarness stability; also apps using zoneless change detection where `Router`/`HttpClient` add `PendingTasks` — tests now wait for them. ZoneJS apps are unchanged by default unless they opt in via `usePendingTasksForStability`.

**Action:** required: re-verify E2E/integration test timing — zoneless tests now wait for routing, HTTP requests, and other `PendingTasks` work before `whenStable` resolves, so previously flaky/passing-too-early tests may now wait (verify timeouts still pass). ZoneJS apps wanting the same behaviour must pass the option to `provideProtractorTestingSupport({usePendingTasksForStability: true})` in the platform providers.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before (v21.2)
export function provideProtractorTestingSupport(): Provider[];

// after (v22.0.0)
export function provideProtractorTestingSupport(options?: {
    usePendingTasksForStability?: boolean;
}): Provider[];

// isStable() before
return this._isZoneStable && !this._ngZone.hasPendingMacrotasks;
// isStable() after
return (
  this._isZoneStable &&
  !this._ngZone.hasPendingMacrotasks &&
  (!this._usePendingTasks || !this.pendingTasksInternal.hasPendingTasks)
);
```

**What changed:** `@Service()` with a `factory` option previously only accepted concrete classes; applying it to an `abstract class` produced a compilation error. The `ServiceDecorator` signature in `packages/core/src/di/service.ts` was widened from `<C extends Type<unknown>>(target: C) => Type<T>` to `<C extends Type<unknown> | AbstractType<unknown>>(target: C) => C extends Type<unknown> ? Type<T> : abstract new (...args: any[]) => T` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/c72ebcb1ad71c99bd1daa82e6448c7d70506b8b3.patch, cherry-picked in https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8ebae1de330729f945391283e25661aada11b4ed). When applied to an abstract class, the decorator now returns `abstract new (...args: any[]) => T` instead of `Type<T>`; the public API golden `goldens/public-api/core/index.api.md` reflects this change to `ServiceDecorator`.

**Affects:** Applications that decorate an `abstract class` with `@Service({factory: ...})` (optionally with `autoProvided?: true`) — these previously failed to compile. Code applying `@Service({factory})` to concrete classes is unchanged in behavior.

**Action:** none — this is a type-level fix that removes a prior compilation error; no code, configuration, or dependency change is required. Applications that previously worked around the error (e.g. casting) may re-verify they can now decorate abstract classes directly.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// ServiceDecorator overload in @angular/core (packages/core/src/di/service.ts)
// Before:
<T>(options: {
    autoProvided?: true;
    factory: () => T;
}): <C extends Type<unknown>>(target: C) => Type<T>;
// After:
<T>(options: {
    autoProvided?: true;
    factory: () => T;
}): <C extends Type<unknown> | AbstractType<unknown>>(target: C) => C extends Type<unknown> ? Type<T> : abstract new (...args: any[]) => T;
```

**What changed:** In 21.2 the shim helpers in `packages/core/src/defer/idle_service.ts` bound `globalThis` only to the real `requestIdleCallback`/`cancelIdleCallback`, leaving the `setTimeout`/`clearTimeout` fallbacks unbound; Safari (and any engine where the fallback runs) then invoked the shim without a proper `window` receiver, breaking `@defer (on idle)` triggers. The fix rewrites the two helpers so the bind applies to whichever function is selected: `(typeof requestIdleCallback !== 'undefined' ? requestIdleCallback : setTimeout).bind(globalThis)` and the same for `cancelIdleCallback`/`clearTimeout` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/f9d8da69243ae1cd0eb1ab197fdd80e9a34107c1.patch). This is a regression fix for the idle-service customization feature added in `a5981b8`, which introduced the public `IdleService` interface (`requestOnIdle(callback: (deadline?: IdleDeadline) => void): number`, `cancelOnIdle(id: number): void`), the `IDLE_SERVICE` injection token, and `provideIdleServiceWith(...)` (). The default `RequestIdleCallbackService` uses these bound shims and is what `IdleScheduler` injects via `IDLE_SERVICE`.

**Affects:** Applications using `@defer` blocks with `on idle` triggers (including the implicit `on idle` default trigger) running in browsers without `requestIdleCallback`, notably Safari; also tests that stubbed `IdleScheduler.requestIdleCallbackFn` (now replaced by injecting `IDLE_SERVICE`).

**Action:** none — no application code, config, or dependency change is required; re-verify that `@defer` blocks with `on idle` still load in Safari-based browsers, and that any custom `IdleService` supplied via `provideIdleServiceWith` still receives `requestOnIdle`/`cancelOnIdle` calls.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// before (21.2)
const _requestIdleCallback = () =>
  typeof requestIdleCallback !== 'undefined' ? requestIdleCallback.bind(globalThis) : setTimeout;
const _cancelIdleCallback = () =>
  typeof requestIdleCallback !== 'undefined' ? cancelIdleCallback.bind(globalThis) : clearTimeout;

// after (22.0)
const _requestIdleCallback = () =>
  (typeof requestIdleCallback !== 'undefined' ? requestIdleCallback : setTimeout).bind(globalThis);
const _cancelIdleCallback = () =>
  (typeof requestIdleCallback !== 'undefined' ? cancelIdleCallback : clearTimeout).bind(globalThis);
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/core`, `registerDomTrigger` (in `packages/core/src/defer/dom_triggers.ts`) now bails out early via `shouldTriggerDeferBlock(type, initialLView)` when the defer config's `behavior` is `DeferBlockBehavior.Manual` — previously DOM triggers (`on viewport`, `on interaction`, `on immmediate`/`on hover` etc., i.e. `ɵɵdeferOnViewport` and friends) registered observers/`afterEveryRender` polls even in manual mode, while timer triggers already skipped (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/61a48e99aad1152e9ffb2fd0b4e1b472f06649e8). `shouldTriggerDeferBlock` was moved from `packages/core/src/defer/triggering.ts` to `packages/core/src/defer/rendering.ts` and now also keeps the existing rule that `TriggerType.Regular` triggers are skipped in `ngServerMode` (same URL). This fixes issue #68800, where `TestBed.configureTestingModule({ deferBlockBehavior: DeferBlockBehavior.Manual })` with `@defer (on viewport)` threw `ReferenceError: IntersectionObserver is not defined` in Jest + JSDOM, polluting stderr via `ErrorHandler` (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/68800).

**Affects:** Applications/tests using `DeferBlockBehavior.Manual` (via `deferBlockBehavior` in `TestBed.configureTestingModule`) with components containing `@defer` blocks that have DOM triggers such as `on viewport`, `on interaction`, or `on hover`.

**Action:** none — for most apps; behavior now matches documented manual-rendering semantics. Tests that asserted observers/listeners were attached, or that relied on the `IntersectionObserver is not defined` stderr noise, must be re-verified. Manual rendering with `fixture.getDeferBlocks()` + `deferBlock.render(DeferBlockState.Complete)` still works and now attaches no observers.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// after fix (packages/core/src/defer/dom_triggers.ts)
export function registerDomTrigger<O>(
  initialLView: LView,
  registerTrigger: RegisterTriggerFunction,
  deferBlockCache: TDeferBlockDetails,
  type: TriggerType,
  options?: O,
) {
  if (!shouldTriggerDeferBlock(type, initialLView)) {
    return;
  }
  ...
```
```ts
// new behavior in tests: no observer is created
TestBed.configureTestingModule({ deferBlockBehavior: DeferBlockBehavior.Manual });
const fixture = TestBed.createComponent(MyCmp); // template has @defer (on viewport(trigger))
fixture.detectChanges();
expect(activeObservers.length).toBe(0);
const deferBlock = (await fixture.getDeferBlocks())[0];
await deferBlock.render(DeferBlockState.Complete);
expect(activeObservers.length).toBe(0);
```

**What changed:** The `factory` option of the `@Service` decorator in `@angular/core` now has a typed return value. The `ServiceDecorator` signature changed from `(options?: {autoProvided?: true; factory?: () => unknown}): TypeDecorator` to a generic overload `<T>(options: {autoProvided?: true; factory: () => T}): <C extends Type<unknown>>(target: C) => Type<T>`, so applying `@Service({factory: ...})` now yields a `Type<T>` for the factory's returned type rather than a plain `TypeDecorator`. The `autoProvided: false` overload also became non-optional (`(options: {autoProvided: false})`), and a separate no-factory overload `(options?: {autoProvided?: true}): TypeDecorator` was added (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/49748b5c7989b4e27686798ea7935e87d804eece). A side effect visible in the updated tests: a factory returning an object with `readonly` properties no longer type-checks against a class with those properties, since `readonly` members are not assignable to mutable ones (Angular's own tests had to change `readonly value = 'MyService'` to `value = 'MyService'`).

**Affects:** Applications using `@Service({factory: ...})` (with or without `autoProvided: true`) where the factory's return type is not structurally assignable to the decorated class — e.g. the factory returns a subset of the class's members, returns a type with `readonly` properties while the class declares them mutable, or where the return type was previously widened by `unknown`.

**Action:** required: re-verify compilation of every class decorated with `@Service({factory: ...})`; if `ng build`/`ng test` reports type errors on the decorator, adjust the factory's returned object to include all class members and to match their mutability (drop `readonly` where the class property is mutable, or vice versa), or cast/align the factory return type with the class. No configuration or dependency change is described in the sources.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (21.2): factory return type was unchecked
@Service({factory: () => ({value: 'alternate'})})
class MyService {
  readonly value = 'MyService';
}

// After (22.0): factory must return T assignable to the class
@Service({factory: () => ({value: 'alternate'})})
class MyService {
  value = 'MyService';
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 22 fixes how signal-forms custom controls are detected when the `value`/`valueChange` (or `checked`/`checkedChange`) and native attributes like `disabled` are not declared directly on the control directive but are exposed through host directives, including transitively nested host directives (`@Component({hostDirectives: [IntermediaryDir]})` where `IntermediaryDir` itself has `hostDirectives: [{directive: BaseControlDir, inputs: ['disabled', 'value'], outputs: ['valueChange']}]`). Internally, `initializeCustomControlStatus` now skips host-directive defs that are not in `tNode.directiveToIndex` and falls back to matching `tNode.hostDirectiveInputs` / `tNode.hostDirectiveOutputs` (`value`/`valueChange`, `checked`/`checkedChange`) to set `TNodeFlags.isFormValueControl` / `TNodeFlags.isFormCheckboxControl` and `tNode.customControlIndex`; `customControlHasInput` now walks all applied host directives recursively (new private `_buildCustomControlInputCache`, helper `getHostDirectives`) and caches the result in a new `DirectiveDef` field `signalFormsInputPresence: Record<string, boolean> | null`; model writes/listeners go through `setDirectiveInput` and `listenToDirectiveOutput` instead of `writeToDirectiveInput`/index-based `listenToOutput`, and host-directive output/input mapping (e.g. `inputs: ['state: value'], outputs: ['stateChange: valueChange']`) plus inputs with a `transform` (e.g. `input(false, {transform: booleanAttribute})`) are now honored (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/16adbbf4234cc67507f578e588a8500fc5d31013 and its .patch).

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms (`form()` / `FormField` in `@angular/forms/signals`) with custom controls implementing `FormValueControl` whose `value`, `checked`, or `disabled` bindings are exposed via `hostDirectives`, especially nested/transitive host directives or aliased/mapped inputs and outputs.

**Action:** none — this is a bug fix in `@angular/core` internals; no application code, template, or dependency changes are required. Re-verify that custom controls bound with `[formField]` still sync value and disabled state when they rely on host-directive aliasing, since previously-undetected controls may now bind and behave differently.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Newly supported pattern (test added in the commit):
@Directive()
class BaseControlDir implements FormValueControl<boolean> {
  readonly value = model(false);
  readonly disabled = input(false);
}

@Directive({
  hostDirectives: [
    {directive: BaseControlDir, inputs: ['disabled', 'value'], outputs: ['valueChange']},
  ],
})
class IntermediaryDir {}

@Component({
  selector: 'custom-transitive-control',
  template: '',
  hostDirectives: [IntermediaryDir],
})
class CustomControl {}
```

**What changed:** The internal implementation of the `debounced` signal/resource helper in `@angular/core` (file `packages/core/src/resource/debounce.ts`) was changed so its internal state is no longer eagerly initialized. Previously, `debounced<T>()` created the state with `signal<ResourceSnapshot<T>>({status: 'resolved', value: untracked(() => source())})`, which eagerly read the `source` signal at creation time. When `debounced(...)` was constructed inside a computation reactive graph — e.g. a signal forms `validateAsync` factory (`packages/forms/signals`, with `form`, `FormField`, `resource({params: ({chain}) => chain(debounce), loader})`) — this eager read of the source dependency could throw a computation cycle error before the enclosing graph node finished computing. The fix replaces the eager `signal` with a lazy `linkedSignal<{value: T; thrown: false} | {error: unknown; thrown: true}, ResourceSnapshot<T>>({source, computation})`, so the source is only read when needed; the effect retains the timing/debounce logic, and a thrown source error now yields `{status: 'error', error}` on first evaluation instead of throwing eagerly. The equality short-circuit in the tracking effect now also covers `currentState.status === 'loading'` in addition to `'reloading'` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/50e599e73ec5bb8f483e749d76fff579e33b1670, patch at https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/50e599e73ec5bb8f483e749d76fff579e33b1670.patch).

**Affects:** Applications that call `debounced(...)` (from `@angular/core`) inside a computed/computation reactive context — notably inside a signal forms `validateAsync` `factory` — which previously could fail with a cycle error; also code relying on `debounced` throwing a source error eagerly at construction time (errors are now reported as `status: 'error'` snapshots).

**Action:** none — this is an internal bug fix; no code, API, or configuration changes are required. Applications that previously hit the cycle error when combining `debounced` with `validateAsync`/computed graphs should re-verify that scenario now works.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (Angular 21.2, packages/core/src/resource/debounce.ts)
const state = signal<ResourceSnapshot<T>>({
  status: 'resolved',
  value: untracked(() => {
    try {
      setInParamsFunction(true);
      return source();
    } finally {
      setInParamsFunction(false);
    }
  }),
});

// After (Angular 22.0)
const state = linkedSignal<
  {value: T; thrown: false} | {error: unknown; thrown: true},
  ResourceSnapshot<T>
>({
  source: () => { /* try { return {value: source(), thrown: false}; } catch ... */ },
  computation: (res, previous) => previous !== undefined
    ? previous.value
    : (res.thrown ? {status: 'error', error: res.error as Error} : {status: 'resolved', value: res.value}),
});
```

**What changed:** An internal Angular core fix in `packages/core/src/render3/util/misc_utils.ts`: `ɵɵresolveWindow` previously had an inferred return type (`Window | null`, from `element.ownerDocument.defaultView`), which did not match the target type expected by the `ɵɵlistener` instruction; it now returns `EventTarget` and uses a non-null assertion (`element.ownerDocument.defaultView!`). Explicit `EventTarget` return-type annotations were also added to `ɵɵresolveDocument` and `ɵɵresolveBody`, and the `ɵɵresolveBody` call site in `packages/core/src/hydration/node_lookup_utils.ts` (`locateRNodeByPath`) was cast `as Element` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/22f8b0a500807e69b323378b843465a949e08abf, patch at .../commit/22f8b0a500807e69b323378b843465a949e08abf.patch). All three functions are marked `@codeGenApi` (https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/22f8b0a500807e69b323378b843465a949e08abf/packages/core/src/render3/util/misc_utils.ts).

**Affects:** Nothing in application code. The changed symbols are compiler-generated / internal (`ɵ`-prefixed, `@codeGenApi`); applications only reach them indirectly through `(window:...)`, `(document:...)`, `(body:...)` event listeners in templates. Runtime behaviour of those listeners is unchanged (same values are returned; only typing/assertions changed).

**Action:** none

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before
export function ɵɵresolveWindow(element: RElement & {ownerDocument: Document}) {
  return element.ownerDocument.defaultView;
}
export function ɵɵresolveDocument(element: RElement & {ownerDocument: Document}) { ... }

// after
export function ɵɵresolveWindow(element: RElement & {ownerDocument: Document}): EventTarget {
  return element.ownerDocument.defaultView!;
}
export function ɵɵresolveDocument(element: RElement & {ownerDocument: Document}): EventTarget { ... }
```

**What changed:** `DomElementSchemaRegistry` now strips the `:svg:` and `:math:` namespace prefixes from tag names before querying `SECURITY_SCHEMA` at compile time, so SVG/MathML attribute lookups are prefix-insensitive and match their security contexts (`packages/compiler/src/schema/dom_element_schema_registry.ts`, `dom_security_schema.ts`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/88d138ccc84b839784f59575fddcda3fcaf18d35, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/61a97f22e8cc18c1a0405d9d880b39d2eb61a012.diff). The same commit adds compile-time sanitization/validation of static i18n-extracted attributes via a new template pipeline phase `resolve_i18n_attr_sanitizers`, which wraps extracted attribute expressions in `sanitizeHtml`, `sanitizeStyle`, `sanitizeScript`, `sanitizeUrl`, `sanitizeResourceUrl` or `validateAttribute(expr, tagName, name)` based on the resolved `SecurityContext`. `SecurityContext` (including `ATTRIBUTE_NO_BINDING = 6`) moved from `packages/compiler/src/core.ts` to `packages/compiler/src/schema/dom_security_schema.ts` and is now `@publicApi`; `SECURITY_SCHEMA` is no longer exported from `packages/compiler/src/compiler.ts`, and the case-insensitivity comment/normalization comment in `hasProperty`-style lookups was removed (tag/prop names are still lower-cased).

**Affects:** applications/templates that use SVG or MathML elements with security-sensitive attributes, and templates with static (i18n-extracted) attributes in a security-sensitive context, e.g. `<a i18n-title title="...">` or SVG `xlink:href`; private users of `@angular/compiler` exports `SECURITY_SCHEMA` or `SecurityContext` from `core.ts`.

**Action:** none for typical applications — re-verify that templates with SVG/MathML security-sensitive attributes and i18n'd static attributes still compile and are sanitized/validated at compile time (a previously silently unsanitized i18n attribute may now fail compilation or be wrapped in a sanitizer). Downstream code importing `SECURITY_SCHEMA` from `@angular/compiler` must be removed/adjusted.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// after (packages/compiler/src/template/pipeline/src/phases/resolve_i18n_attr_sanitizers.ts)
switch (op.securityContext) {
  case SecurityContext.URL:
    expr = o.importExpr(Identifiers.sanitizeUrl).callFn([expr]);
    break;
  case SecurityContext.ATTRIBUTE_NO_BINDING:
    expr = o
      .importExpr(Identifiers.validateAttribute)
      .callFn([expr, o.literal(tagName), o.literal(op.name)]);
    break;
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 22 removes SVG-namespaced `<script>` from the security sanitization schema, because such elements are now stripped outright. In PR 68689 (`fix(compiler): strip namespaced SVG script elements during template compilation`), `preparseElement` in `packages/compiler/src/template_parser/template_preparser.ts` matches elements against `SCRIPT_ELEMENTS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([':svg:script', 'script'])` (and `STYLE_ELEMENTS = new Set([':svg:style', 'style'])`), so namespaced SVG `<script>`/`<style>` are treated as SCRIPT/STYLE and stripped during template compilation (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/68689). Commit `bfe6f6c2a5` then deleted the matching `SecurityContext.RESOURCE_URL` entries `['script', ['src', 'href', 'xlink:href']]` from the SVG namespace in `packages/core/src/sanitization/dom_security_schema.ts` (`SECURITY_SCHEMA()`), generated by `pnpm bazel run //packages/core:dom_security_schema`, and the `':svg:script'` / `'script'` `RESOURCE_URL` entries in `RESOURCE_MAP` in `packages/core/src/sanitization/sanitization.ts` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/bfe6f6c2a5570cd669afa3dd8b1cd9e2d91e393a). Consequence: `getUrlSanitizer('script', 'xlink:href')` now returns `ɵɵsanitizeUrl` instead of `ɵɵsanitizeResourceUrl`, and `ɵɵsanitizeUrlOrResourceUrl('javascript:true', 'script', 'xlink:href')` returns `'unsafe:javascript:true'` instead of throwing (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/68689).

**Affects:** templates binding `[attr.href]`, `[attr.xlink:href]` or `[attr.src]` on a `<script>` inside `<svg>`; those elements are removed from rendered output and no longer get `ɵɵsanitizeResourceUrl` in generated code.

**Action:** none — no application code change is required; re-verify any template that intentionally rendered SVG `<script>` (e.g. via `bypassSecurityTrustResourceUrl` bindings), since such elements are now stripped from compiled templates.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// packages/compiler/src/template_parser/template_preparser.ts
-const STYLE_ELEMENT = 'style';
-const SCRIPT_ELEMENT = 'script';
+const STYLE_ELEMENTS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([':svg:style', 'style']);
+const SCRIPT_ELEMENTS: ReadonlySet<string> = new Set([':svg:script', 'script']);

// packages/core/src/sanitization/dom_security_schema.ts (removed by bfe6f6c2a5)
-    registerContext(SecurityContext.RESOURCE_URL, SVG_NAMESPACE, [
-      ['script', ['src', 'href', 'xlink:href']],
-    ]);
```

**What changed:** The internal (codegen) types for directive definition `inputs` and `outputs` were widened so the keys are no longer constrained to the members of the directive class. In `packages/core/src/render3/definition.ts`, `DirectiveInputs<T>` (a mapped type `[P in keyof T]?: string | [flags: InputFlags, publicName: string, declaredName?: string, transform?: InputTransformFunction]`) became the non-generic `type DirectiveInputs = Record<string, string | [flags: InputFlags, publicName: string, declaredName?: string, transform?: InputTransformFunction] | undefined>`, `DirectiveDefinition<T>.inputs?: DirectiveInputs<T>` became `inputs?: DirectiveInputs`, and `outputs?: {[P in keyof T]?: string}` became `outputs?: Record<string, string | undefined>` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/5e99ae9f00fb119cac93a19bbf36aee71299cae1). The reason: the compiler cannot guarantee that generated entries correspond to real class members, e.g. `model` inputs implicitly generate an output, so the type had to accept any string key (same commit).

**Affects:** Only code that reads/writes the private `ɵdir`/`ɵcmp` definition objects through `ɵɵdefineDirective`, `ɵɵdefineComponent` or `DirectiveDefinition`, or that references the `DirectiveInputs<T>` type — i.e. library/compiler/tooling code that manipulates `inputs`/`outputs` maps with generic-typed keys. Ordinary `@Input()`, `@Output()`, `input()`, `output()`, and `model()` usage is unaffected.

**Action:** none — no application source, template, or build change is required. Applications that poke at `ɵdir.inputs` / `ɵdir.outputs` in a type-checked way should re-verify that they compile, since keys and values are now `string`/`string | undefined` instead of `keyof T`.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before
type DirectiveInputs<T> = {
  [P in keyof T]?: string | [flags: InputFlags, publicName: string, declaredName?: string, transform?: InputTransformFunction];
};
// in DirectiveDefinition<T>:
inputs?: DirectiveInputs<T>;
outputs?: {[P in keyof T]?: string};

// after
type DirectiveInputs = Record<
  string,
  string | [flags: InputFlags, publicName: string, declaredName?: string, transform?: InputTransformFunction] | undefined
>;
// in DirectiveDefinition<T>:
inputs?: DirectiveInputs;
outputs?: Record<string, string | undefined>;
```

**What changed:** The public type `AnimationCallbackEvent` (introduced `@publicApi 20.2`, used with `animate.enter` / `animate.leave` function callbacks) had its `animationComplete` member retyped from `Function` to `VoidFunction`, i.e. `() => void` with no accepted arguments and no return value. Final declaration in `packages/core/src/animation/interfaces.ts`: `export type AnimationCallbackEvent = {target: Element; animationComplete: VoidFunction};` (previously `animationComplete: Function`), also reflected in the public API golden `goldens/public-api/core/index.api.md` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/b9b5c279b444ab2684fe911982930dc7c31ed43c.patch, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/main/packages/core/src/animation/interfaces.ts). The related public type `AnimationFunction = (event: AnimationCallbackEvent) => void` is unchanged (same interfaces.ts URL). Motivation: calling a value typed as `Function` is an error under the `typescript-eslint` `no-unsafe-call` rule, forcing rule disables (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/65613). The commit is labelled `BREAKING CHANGE: change AnimationCallbackEvent.animationComplete signature` and fixes #65613.

**Affects:** Applications that consume `AnimationCallbackEvent` (typically via `AnimationFunction` callbacks for `animate.enter` / `animate.leave`) and either call `event.animationComplete(...)` with arguments, use its return value, assign a non-`()`/non-`void`-compatible function to `animationComplete`, or keep their own type/declaration copies mirroring `animationComplete: Function`. No runtime behaviour change — this is a type-level change only.

**Action:** required: re-verify that code reading/calling `animationComplete` passes no arguments and ignores any return value; update any local or duplicated type declarations of `AnimationCallbackEvent` (or of `AnimationFunction` parameters) that still declare `animationComplete: Function` to `animationComplete: VoidFunction`; with `typescript-eslint`, the `@typescript-eslint/no-unsafe-call` disable comments previously needed around `animationComplete()` calls can be removed.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before (Angular 20.2 - 21.2)
export type AnimationCallbackEvent = {target: Element; animationComplete: Function};

// after (Angular 22.0)
export type AnimationCallbackEvent = {target: Element; animationComplete: VoidFunction};
```

**What changed:** `ChangeDetectorRef.checkNoChanges()` was removed from Angular's public API in Angular 22, because it was "an internal API" (commit message: `refactor(core): remove \`checkNoChanges\` from the public API.`). After the change, the abstract class `ChangeDetectorRef` in `packages/core/src/change_detection/change_detector_ref.ts` only declares `markForCheck()`, `detach()`, `detectChanges()`, and `reattach()` — no `checkNoChanges()` member remains (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/69fb1614eff6e40bb7dcca81f275ac32b9cbd28a, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/69fb1614eff6e40bb7dcca81f275ac32b9cbd28a/packages/core/src/change_detection/change_detector_ref.ts). The commit message states the migration: "In tests use `fixture.detectChanges()` instead." This is labelled a breaking change in the release notes for 22.0.0.

**Affects:** applications or specs that call `checkNoChanges()` on an injected `ChangeDetectorRef` (or implement/override `ChangeDetectorRef.checkNoChanges`), typically in unit tests that ran a second change-detection pass to assert stability.

**Action:** required: delete calls to `ChangeDetectorRef.checkNoChanges()`; in test code replace them with `fixture.detectChanges()`. Remove any class member implementations of `checkNoChanges` that satisfied the `ChangeDetectorRef` contract.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (Angular <= 21.x): second pass asserting no further changes
changeDetectorRef.checkNoChanges();

// After (Angular 22): use the fixture instead
fixture.detectChanges();
```

**What changed:** The deprecated public API `createNgModuleRef` was removed from `@angular/core`. Previously it was a deprecated alias: `export const createNgModuleRef = createNgModule;` (`@public @deprecated`). The export was deleted from `packages/core/src/core.ts` and the public API golden (`goldens/public-api/core/index.api.md`), leaving only `createNgModule<T>(ngModule: Type<T>, parentInjector?: Injector): NgModuleRef<T>` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/36936872c962b2073c8f44080684701068866691, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/36936872c962b2073c8f44080684701068866691/packages/core/src/render3/ng_module_ref.ts, https://raw.githubusercontent.com/angular/angular/36936872c962b2073c8f44080684701068866691/goldens/public-api/core/index.api.md)

**Affects:** Applications and libraries that import `createNgModuleRef` from `@angular/core`.

**Action:** required: remove `createNgModuleRef` from all `import { ... } from '@angular/core'` statements and replace every `createNgModuleRef(...)` call site with `createNgModule(...)`. No signature changes are required: both take `(ngModule: Type<T>, parentInjector?: Injector)` and return `NgModuleRef<T>`.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before
import { createNgModuleRef } from '@angular/core';
// @public @deprecated
export const createNgModuleRef: typeof createNgModule;

// After
import { createNgModule } from '@angular/core';
// @public
export function createNgModule<T>(ngModule: Type<T>, parentInjector?: Injector): NgModuleRef<T>;
```
</answer>

**What changed:** In Angular 22.0.0, `ComponentFactory` and `ComponentFactoryResolver` were removed from the public `@angular/core` API surface — they are no longer exported as `@publicApi`, only internally as `ɵComponentFactory` and `ɵComponentFactoryResolver` in `packages/core/src/linker.ts` ("Those APIs date back to pre-ivy times and are long deprecated.") The deprecated overloads that accepted a factory were also deleted: `ApplicationRef.bootstrap<C>(componentFactory: ComponentFactory<C>, rootSelectorOrNode?: string | any)` is gone, leaving only `bootstrap<C>(component: Type<C>, rootSelectorOrNode?: string | any)`; and `ViewContainerRef.createComponent<C>(componentFactory: ComponentFactory<C>, index?, injector?, projectableNodes?, environmentInjector?, directives?, bindings?)` is gone, leaving the class-based form `createComponent<C>(componentType: Type<C>, opts?: { index?; injector?; ngModuleRef?; environmentInjector?; projectableNodes?; directives?; bindings? })`. Also removed: `ComponentFactoryResolver.NULL` and `resolveComponentFactory` from the public API. Commit message: "Pass the component class directly to APIs that previously required a factory, such as `ViewContainerRef.createComponent` or use the standalone `createComponent` function." (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/9d76ac82290e047f1481fb38bd95233e951a77de)

**Affects:** Applications/libraries that import `ComponentFactory`, `ComponentFactoryResolver` or `ComponentFactoryResolver.NULL` from `@angular/core`, call `resolveComponentFactory(...)`, pass a factory to `ApplicationRef.bootstrap()`, or use the positional-argument (factory) overload of `ViewContainerRef.createComponent()`.

**Action:** required: delete imports of `ComponentFactory` / `ComponentFactoryResolver` from `@angular/core` (the `ɵComponentFactory` / `ɵComponentFactoryResolver` exports are internal-only, not a supported replacement); pass the component class (`Type<C>`) instead of a factory to `ViewContainerRef.createComponent` / `ApplicationRef.bootstrap`, using the options object (`{ index, injector, projectableNodes, environmentInjector, ngModuleRef, directives, bindings }`) rather than positional arguments; or switch to the standalone `createComponent` function. Note the dev-mode assertion that a `number` as the second argument to `createComponent` with a component type is invalid — use an object.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before (removed in v22)
import {ComponentFactory, ComponentFactoryResolver} from '@angular/core';
const factory = resolver.resolveComponentFactory(MyComp);
vcr.createComponent(factory, 0, injector);
appRef.bootstrap(factory);

// After (from the v22 API golden)
abstract createComponent<C>(componentType: Type<C>, opts?: {
  index?: number; injector?: Injector; ngModuleRef?: NgModuleRef<unknown>;
  environmentInjector?: EnvironmentInjector | NgModuleRef<unknown>;
  projectableNodes?: Node[][];
  directives?: (Type<unknown> | DirectiveWithBindings<unknown>)[];
  bindings?: Binding[];
}): ComponentRef<C>;
bootstrap<C>(component: Type<C>, rootSelectorOrNode?: string | any): ComponentRef<C>;
```

**What changed:** In Angular 22.0.0, the pre-IVY factory APIs were removed from `@angular/core`'s public API surface (commit b1f5181ffd). `ComponentFactory` and `ComponentFactoryResolver` (including `ComponentFactoryResolver.NULL` and `resolveComponentFactory<T>(component: Type<T>)`) are no longer exported as public API; the exports in `packages/core/src/linker.ts` were renamed to the private `ɵComponentFactory` / `ɵComponentFactoryResolver`, and their `@publicApi`/`@deprecated` markers were dropped. The deprecated factory-based overloads were deleted: `ViewContainerRef.createComponent(componentFactory, index?, injector?, projectableNodes?, environmentInjector?, directives?, bindings?)` and `ApplicationRef.bootstrap(componentFactory, rootSelectorOrNode?)`; `ApplicationRef.bootstrap` now only accepts `bootstrap<C>(component: Type<C>, rootSelectorOrNode?: string | any)` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/b1f5181ffd8e9906affd486d9e2f655eb144f175). Internal consumers `@angular/upgrade` (`component-factory-strategy.ts`, `downgrade_component.ts`, `downgrade_component_adapter.ts`) and `@angular/elements` (`utils.ts`) were adjusted in the same commit.

**Affects:** Applications/libraries that import `ComponentFactory` or `ComponentFactoryResolver` from `@angular/core`, call `resolveComponentFactory(...)`, use `ComponentFactory.create(...)`, pass a factory to `ViewContainerRef.createComponent` or `ApplicationRef.bootstrap`, or inject `ComponentFactoryResolver`.

**Action:** required: remove imports of `ComponentFactory` / `ComponentFactoryResolver` from `@angular/core`; pass the component class directly to `ViewContainerRef.createComponent` (with the options object form, e.g. `{index, injector, environmentInjector, projectableNodes, ngModuleRef, directives, bindings}`) or use the standalone `createComponent` function; pass the component class to `ApplicationRef.bootstrap`; replace positional-argument (index/injector/projectableNodes/environmentInjector) calls with the options-object signature; re-verify `@angular/upgrade` hybrid downgrade/upgrade-component behavior.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// Before (removed in v22)
abstract createComponent<C>(
  componentFactory: ComponentFactory<C>,
  index?: number,
  injector?: Injector,
  projectableNodes?: any[][],
  environmentInjector?: EnvironmentInjector | NgModuleRef<any>,
  directives?: (Type<unknown> | DirectiveWithBindings<unknown>)[],
  bindings?: Binding[],
): ComponentRef<C>;

// After
bootstrap<C>(component: Type<C>, rootSelectorOrNode?: string | any): ComponentRef<C>;
createComponent<C>(
  componentType: Type<C>,
  opts?: {
    index?: number; injector?: Injector; ngModuleRef?: NgModuleRef<unknown>;
    environmentInjector?: EnvironmentInjector | NgModuleRef<unknown>;
    projectableNodes?: Node[][]; directives?: (Type<unknown> | DirectiveWithBindings<unknown>)[];
    bindings?: Binding[];
  },
): ComponentRef<C>;
```

**What changed:** Signal Forms gain a new public API method `reloadValidation(): void` on the `FieldState` interface (alongside `markAsDirty()`, `markAsTouched()`, `reset()`, `value`, `fieldTree`), which "Reloads all asynchronous validators for this field and its descendants." It recursively walks the form tree and calls `reload()` on the underlying `ResourceRef` for every metadata key tagged with the new exported `IS_ASYNC_VALIDATION_RESOURCE: unique symbol` (marked `@experimental 21.0.0`); `validateAsync` (and thus `validateHttp`) now tags its internal `RESOURCE` metadata key with this symbol (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/74f76d8075d03b1271aef37b974c9e15f9c7d3af.patch). This is the Signal Forms analogue of Reactive Forms' `updateValueAndValidity`, requested because non-idempotent async validators can be stale by form submission time (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/66994). It is an additive feature, not a breaking change.

**Affects:** Applications using Signal Forms (`@angular/forms/signals`) with asynchronous validation via `validateAsync` / `validateHttp` that need manual re-validation; no existing code changes behaviour.

**Action:** none — the new method is opt-in; call `form()().reloadValidation()` where re-validation of async rules is desired (e.g. just before submission).

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// FieldState (goldens/public-api/forms/signals/index.api.md)
readonly fieldTree: FieldTree<unknown, TKey>;
markAsDirty(): void;
markAsTouched(options?: MarkAsTouchedOptions): void;
reloadValidation(): void;
reset(value?: TValue): void;
readonly value: WritableSignal<TValue>;

// new export
export const IS_ASYNC_VALIDATION_RESOURCE: unique symbol;

// usage (test/node/resource.spec.ts)
usernameForm().reloadValidation(); // re-issues the validateHttp request
```

**What changed:** Angular 22 adds an optional `debounce` property to the signals-forms async validator options in `@angular/forms/signals`: `AsyncValidatorOptions.debounce?: DebounceTimer<TParams | undefined>` (used by `validateAsync`) and `HttpValidatorOptions.debounce?: DebounceTimer<string | HttpResourceRequest | undefined>` (used by `validateHttp`) — "Duration in milliseconds to wait before triggering the async operation, or a function that returns a promise that resolves when the update should proceed." A new public type was also exported from `@angular/core`: `export type DebounceTimer<T> = number | ((value: T, lastValue: ResourceSnapshot<T>) => Promise<void> | void);`, which also replaces the inline union in the `wait` parameter of `debounced<T>(source, wait, options?)` in `@angular/core` (same external signature, now typed via `DebounceTimer<T>`). Internally `validateAsync` wraps params in `debounced(() => params(), opts.debounce)` when `debounce` is set, and `validateHttp` forwards `debounce` to `validateAsync` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/24e52d450d201e3da90bb64f84358f9eccd7877d).

**Affects:** Nothing for existing code — additive opt-in API; only apps that want debounced async/HTTP validation in signal forms (`validateAsync`, `validateHttp`) use it. The `debounced` change is type-alias only, not breaking.

**Action:** none (optional: add `debounce` to `validateAsync`/`validateHttp` options to throttle validator requests).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// after (22.0) — @angular/forms/signals
import {DebounceTimer} from '@angular/core';

export interface HttpValidatorOptions<TValue, TResult, TPathKind extends PathKind = PathKind.Root> {
    readonly debounce?: DebounceTimer<string | HttpResourceRequest | undefined>;
    // ...onError, onSuccess, options, request
}

validateHttp(p, {
  request: ({value}) => `/api/check?username=${value()}`,
  debounce: 50, // Short debounce
  onSuccess: (available: boolean) => (available ? undefined : {kind: 'username-taken'}),
  onError: () => null,
});
```

**What changed:** Angular 22 shims legacy `NG_VALIDATORS` into signal-forms `FormField` parse errors when a component is bound via the `FormField` directive in ControlValueAccessor mode. `cvaControlCreate` now reads `parent.injector.get(NG_VALIDATORS, null, {optional: true, self: true})`, wraps each entry (functions cast to `ValidatorFn`, `Validator` objects via `v.validate.bind(v)`) with `Validators.compose`, and sets a `computed` signal into `parent.parseErrorsSource` that runs the merged validator against `parent.interopNgControl.control` and maps results through `reactiveErrorsToSignalErrors` (`packages/forms/signals/src/directive/control_cva.ts`). To keep it reactive, `registerOnValidatorChange` on each object validator bumps an internal `version` signal, so validation re-runs even when the model value is unchanged (e.g. `null` → `null`). `FormField.parseErrorsSource` changed from `private` to `readonly` and `FormField.interopNgControl` from `protected get` to `get` (both `@internal`; removed from the public API goldens). Companion commit `72d3ace03c` in the same PR (#67943) makes the interop `NgControl` report `controlValue()` instead of `value()`, so CVA code sees the latest user input immediately instead of after debounced synchronization (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/f9f24fc6699b762d17127d0412343041ecdea70e.patch, https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/72d3ace03c1292ba9d6fdf7b418ba3287bf54316)

**Affects:** Applications using signal-forms `FormField` (`form(...)` / `[formField]`) bound to custom controls that provide `NG_VALUE_ACCESSOR` and also provide `NG_VALIDATORS` (directive or function validators); also custom CVAs that read `NgControl.value` during input handling when `debounce` is active.

**Action:** required: re-verify validation of signal-form fields wrapping legacy CVA components that provide `NG_VALIDATORS` — their errors now surface in `field().errors()` as parse errors (e.g. `kind: 'legacy-parse'`, `context` holding the original `ValidationErrors` entry), which can change validity/error display and previously silently-passing fields. Verify custom CVAs that read `NgControl.value` inside `registerOnChange`/input handlers now observe the un-debounced value. Re-verify any subclass overriding `FormField.interopNgControl` (visibility changed from `protected` to public `get`, `@internal`).

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Custom CVA control used with signal forms: previously ignored by FormField
providers: [
  {provide: NG_VALUE_ACCESSOR, useExisting: LegacyControlWithValidators, multi: true},
  {provide: NG_VALIDATORS, useExisting: LegacyControlWithValidators, multi: true},
],
validate(control: AbstractControl): ValidationErrors | null {
  return legacyErrors();
}
// after: field().errors() => [{ kind: 'legacy-parse', context: {text: 'bad'} }]
```

**What changed:** Signal-forms `<input type="range">` binding bug fixed: previously `nativeControlCreate` in `packages/forms/signals/src/directive/control_native.ts` called `setNativeControlValue(input, controlValue)` *before* writing `min`/`max` attributes, so the browser clamped the value to the native default range (0–100) and the slider rendered at the wrong position (e.g. value 150 with `min=100`/`max=200` showed at the start). The fix moves the `state.controlValue()` / `setNativeControlValue` call to after the `CONTROL_BINDING_NAMES` attribute loop, with the comment "We need to update the value after setting the attributes as some attributes like min/max might prevent from setting the value" (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3524de29f34bef5df941e08e88920dffe4f880c8). Fixes issue #68480 "Signal formField range input with min/max does not correctly render" (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/68480).

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms (`form()` / `formField` from `@angular/forms/signals`) bound to `<input type="range">` where `min`/`max` come from field validators (`min(...)`, `max(...)`); previously the slider position/`input.value` was wrong. Non-signal `FormControl` + `Validators.min/max` behaviour is unchanged.

**Action:** none — no code change required; re-verify that signal-form range sliders render at the correct position and that model values still sync correctly (the browser clamps the DOM value to the native min/max, as in the fix's regression test where model `150` with max `100` yields `input.value === '100'`).

**Before/after:**
```html
<!-- before/after template unchanged; only rendering is now correct -->
<input type="range" [formField]="form.range" />
```
```ts
form = form(signal({range: 80}), (path) => {
  min(path.range, this.min);
  max(path.range, this.max);
});
```

**What changed:** The `FormRoot` directive (`packages/forms/signals/src/directive/form_root.ts`) in the `@angular/forms/signals` (signal forms) package previously always invoked `submit(fieldTree)` inside its `onSubmit(event)` host handler on every form `submit` event, which threw runtime error `NG01915: Cannot submit form with no submit action. Specify the action when creating the form, or as an additional argument to `submit().` when the bound `FieldTree` had no submit options defined (https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/67367). In 22.0.0 the handler wraps the call in `untracked()` and now checks `if (node.structure.fieldManager.submitOptions) { submit(fieldTree); }` (with `node = fieldTree() as FieldState<unknown> as FieldNode`), so `submit()` is only called when the form defines its own submission options (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0eeb1b5f03395ea0ddb047790af4cf1440655a07). The directive still always sets `novalidate` on the `<form>` and calls `event.preventDefault()` on the `submit` event (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/67727).

**Affects:** Applications using the signal-forms `FormRoot` directive (`<form [formRoot]="fieldTree">`) where the bound `FieldTree` does not define its own submission options (submit action).

**Action:** required: re-verify form submission behaviour. Forms that previously triggered the `NG01915` error now silently skip `submit()`; if a submission is still expected, either define submission options on the form/`FieldTree`, or call `submit()` manually in application code (e.g. via `(submit)` handler or a button handler). `FormRoot` can now be used solely for its default behavior of setting `novalidate` and calling `preventDefault()`.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// form_root.ts onSubmit handler
// Before (21.2.x):
event.preventDefault();
submit(this.fieldTree());

// After (22.0.0):
event.preventDefault();
untracked(() => {
  const fieldTree = this.fieldTree();
  const node = fieldTree() as FieldState<unknown> as FieldNode;
  if (node.structure.fieldManager.submitOptions) {
    submit(fieldTree);
  }
});
```

**What changed:** In the signals forms `FormField` directive (`packages/forms/signals/src/directive/form_field.ts`), the helper flag used to decide whether `min`/`max` validators should be written to the native element (`elementAcceptsNumericValues`, computed by `isNumericFormElement(this.element)`) was previously evaluated eagerly once during `FormField` construction. When an `<input>`'s `type` was bound late (e.g. `<input [type]="inputType()" [formField]="f" />`), the type was not yet resolved at construction time, so the numeric check failed and constraint attributes such as `min`/`max` were never applied (issue #66987). The fix makes this check lazy: the value is now stored in a `_elementAcceptsNumericValues: boolean | undefined` field and computed on first use inside the `shouldApplyConstraint`-style switch (`case 'min': case 'max': return (this._elementAcceptsNumericValues ??= isNumericFormElement(this.element));`), so late-bound `[type]` bindings evaluated during initialization are picked up and `min`/`max` (and textual constraints `minLength`/`maxLength`, which use `isTextFormElement`/`elementAcceptsTextualValues`) are applied correctly (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/394ad0c2a26eec8a8f7136b1b7971420b30a117e, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/66987).

**Affects:** Applications using signals forms `FormField` (`[formField]`) on a native `<input>` whose `type` is bound via a property binding (e.g. `[type]="someSignal()"` or other late-bound expression), combined with `min`/`max` or `minLength`/`maxLength` validators in the form schema. Previously those HTML constraint attributes were silently missing on such inputs.

**Action:** none — this is an internal bug fix with no API change; upgrading applications need no code changes. Only re-verify that inputs with dynamic `[type]` now receive the expected `min`/`max`/`maxlength` attributes (browser-native validation may now kick in where it previously did not).

**Before/after:**
```html
<!-- FormField with a late-bound input type -->
<input [type]="inputType()" [formField]="f" />
```
```ts
readonly inputType = signal('number');
readonly min = signal(10);
readonly f = form(signal(15), (p) => {
  min(p, this.min);
});

// before: input.min was not applied (late-bound type was missed)
// after (per added spec): expect(input.min).toBe('10');
```

**What changed:** In `@angular/forms/signals`, four properties on `ReadonlyFieldState<TValue, TKey>` (and thus `FieldState`) changed from optional (`?`) to required-but-possibly-`undefined`: `readonly max?: Signal<number | undefined>` → `readonly max: Signal<number | undefined> | undefined`, and likewise `maxLength`, `min`, `minLength`. This makes the types compatible with TypeScript's `exactOptionalPropertyTypes`, fixing `Class 'FieldNode' incorrectly implements interface 'FieldState<unknown, string | number>'` compile errors (issue #67246, commit `ee8d2098cb`) (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ee8d2098cb3cdce1589c462cd9a66eae490477f9). The same commit also changes the `create` callback of `createManagedMetadataKey` and `MetadataKey.create` from `(s: Signal<TAcc>) => TRead` to `(state: FieldState<unknown>, data: Signal<TAcc>) => TRead` — the state is now the first argument and creation runs at the end of `FieldNode` construction via `runMetadataCreateLifecycle()` (internal, but observable as an extra first parameter).

**Affects:** Applications using `@angular/forms/signals` that (a) implement/assign their own object to `FieldState`/`ReadonlyFieldState`, or (b) pass a `create` callback to `createManagedMetadataKey` (including indirectly via `validateAsync`'s `opts.factory` resource pattern).

**Action:** required: update custom `create` callbacks for `createManagedMetadataKey` to accept the new leading `state: FieldState<unknown>` parameter (e.g. `(_state, params) => ...`); update code that implements `FieldState`/`ReadonlyFieldState` or narrows on presence of `max`/`maxLength`/`min`/`minLength` to handle explicitly `undefined` values instead of missing keys. No changes needed for ordinary `form()`, `required`, `submit`, `metadata()` usage.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// ReadonlyFieldState properties (before → after)
- readonly max?: Signal<number | undefined>;
- readonly maxLength?: Signal<number | undefined>;
+ readonly max: Signal<number | undefined> | undefined;
+ readonly maxLength: Signal<number | undefined> | undefined;

// createManagedMetadataKey create callback
- const RES = createManagedMetadataKey((params: Signal<{x: string} | undefined>) =>
+ const RES = createManagedMetadataKey((_state, params: Signal<{x: string} | undefined>) =>
    resource({params, loader: async ({params}) => `got: ${params.x}`})
  );
```
(https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/ee8d2098cb3cdce1589c462cd9a66eae490477f9)

**What changed:** A bug fix in signal forms (`@angular/forms/signals`): previously, when a user cleared a native date-like input via the browser's internal UI, the element's validity changed without an `input` event, so the form model kept the old value and showed a `NativeInputParseError` parse error (issue #67300, regression introduced in 21.2.0). Angular 22 introduces an injectable `InputValidityMonitor` (abstract class with implementation `AnimationInputValidityMonitor`, provided in root) in `packages/forms/signals/src/directive/input_validity_monitor.ts`. It dynamically injects a CSP-nonce-aware `<style>` tag (per Document/ShadowRoot) with `@keyframes ng-valid {}` / `@keyframes ng-invalid {}` applied to `input:valid`, `textarea:valid`, `input:invalid`, `textarea:invalid` (`animation: ng-valid 0.001s;`), and listens for `animationstart` events named `ng-valid`/`ng-invalid` to re-invoke the value parser. Monitoring is only installed for `<input>` elements whose `type` is `date`, `datetime-local`, `month`, `time`, or `week` (helper `inputRequiresValidityTracking`), and `element.validity.badInput` checks now go through `validityMonitor.isBadInput(element)` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/df8b020299b5e579956578d9137cab93a8065045 and its .diff).

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms (`FormField` / `form()` from `@angular/forms/signals`) bound to native `<input type="date">`, `datetime-local`, `month`, `time`, or `week` controls; also apps whose CSS could be influenced by the injected `ng-valid`/`ng-invalid` keyframes, and custom test doubles relying on `validity.badInput` (now overridable via `InputValidityMonitor`).

**Action:** none — the fix is automatic; optionally re-verify that clearing date/time inputs in signal forms now propagates an empty value and clears parse errors, and that app styles relying on `animation` on `input`/`textarea` are unaffected by the injected keyframes.

**Before/after:**
```css
/* Style tag Angular now injects into document.head (or the ShadowRoot): */
@keyframes ng-valid {}
@keyframes ng-invalid {}
input:valid, textarea:valid {
  animation: ng-valid 0.001s;
}
input:invalid, textarea:invalid {
  animation: ng-invalid 0.001s;
}
```

**What changed:** Angular 22 fixes Signal Forms' `ControlValueAccessor` interop so that a view→model change no longer triggers a redundant `writeValue` back to the CVA. Previously, when a CVA bound with `[formField]` called its registered `onChange` fn, `packages/forms/signals/src/directive/control_cva.ts` only did `parent.state().controlValue.set(value)`, so the binding effect saw the model value as "updated" and pushed it straight back into `writeValue` on the same component — contradicting the documented one-way `ControlValueAccessor` contract (`writeValue` is only for programmatic model→view changes) and, in reported cases, causing an infinite loop (`#67847`). Now `cvaControlCreate` creates the `bindings` object earlier and the `registerOnChange` callback sets `bindings['controlValue'] = value` before `parent.state().controlValue.set(value)`, so `bindingUpdated(bindings, 'controlValue', value)` returns false and the write-back is skipped (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/2e9aeea0fed1a2eae261b95cb1479519d0428b83, https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/67847).

**Affects:** applications using Signal Forms `FormField` (`[formField]`) interop with a custom `ControlValueAccessor` component, especially CVAs whose `writeValue` mutates state that can re-trigger `onChange`, or that rely on counting/observing `writeValue` calls.

**Action:** none — no code, template, or dependency change is required. This is an internal framework fix; only re-verify custom CVAs used with `[formField]` to confirm they do not depend on receiving an immediate echo of their own `onChange` value via `writeValue`.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// control_cva.ts — before
parent.controlValueAccessor!.registerOnChange((value: unknown) =>
  parent.state().controlValue.set(value as any),
);
// ...
const bindings = createBindings<ControlBindingKey | 'controlValue'>();

// control_cva.ts — after
const bindings = createBindings<ControlBindingKey | 'controlValue'>();

parent.controlValueAccessor!.registerOnChange((value: unknown) => {
  // Prevents the framework from writing the same value back to the CVA (CVA loopback).
  bindings['controlValue'] = value;
  parent.state().controlValue.set(value as any);
});
```

**What changed:** In signal forms, `validateAsync` with a `debounce` option now reports the field as pending during the debounce wait itself, not only after the debounce completes and the resource starts loading. Previously the internal `debounced(() => params(), opts.debounce)` resource's loading status was not propagated to the resource built by the user's `factory` (which received `debouncedResource.value`), so `field().pending()` stayed `false` and stale validation errors remained visible for the whole debounce window (). The fix passes `computed(() => ɵchain(debouncedResource))` to the factory instead, replicating resource `params` "chain" semantics; `chain` was extracted from `paramsContext` in `packages/core/src/resource/resource.ts` and privately exported as `ɵchain` from `packages/core/src/core_private_export.ts` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/0ea50ffe5adb07515867e8bf30d1abee49413003). The async error rule maps resource statuses `loading`/`reloading` to `'pending'`, so the field now enters pending as soon as the value changes and the debounce timer starts.

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms `validateAsync` (from `@angular/forms/signals`) with the `debounce` option set in `AsyncValidatorOptions`, especially those relying on `pending()`/`invalid()` timing in tests or UI (spinners, error display).

**Action:** none — no public API, property, or dependency change; but re-verify any UI or tests that observed `field().pending()` remaining `false` (and stale errors persisting) during the debounce period, since pending now begins immediately on value change.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before (21.2): factory got the debounced value signal; debounce not reflected as pending
const debouncedResource = debounced(() => params(), opts.debounce);
return opts.factory(debouncedResource.value);

// after (22.0): chain propagates the debounced resource's loading status
const debouncedResource = debounced(() => params(), opts.debounce);
const wrappedParams = computed(() => ɵchain(debouncedResource));
return opts.factory(wrappedParams);
```

**What changed:** A bug fix in signal forms (`@angular/forms/signals`): when an item is deleted from a model array, its DOM element is removed during change detection, which fires a synchronous `blur` event; the blur handler called `FieldNode.markAsTouched()`, which navigated up through `keyInParent`, throwing `RuntimeError: orphan field, can't find element in array <name>` (e.g. `<root>.items`) because the item was already gone from the array in signals state (github.com/angular/angular/commit/3c44d7c90b2392f7307d1b1dd0734db10ede63f5, issue https://github.com/angular/angular/issues/66711). The fix adds an abstract `isOrphaned: Signal<boolean>` to `FieldNodeStructure` (with `ChildFieldNodeStructure`, `RootFieldNodeStructure`, and `CompatStructure` in the signals compat package implementing it), created by a new `createKeyOrOrphanSignals` method replacing `createKeyInParent`, using an internal `ORPHAN_TOKEN` and a reactivity-insulated `childrenMap` poll; `FieldNode.markAsTouched()` and `markAsTouchedInternal()` now return early when `this.structure.isOrphaned()` is true (patch in commit).

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms (`form()`, `FormField`) that render array items with `[formField]` bindings in a `@for` loop where a focused input's item can be removed from the model array — previously these threw unhandled orphan-field `RuntimeError`s (also seen when tracking with e.g. `track child.id()`).

**Action:** none — this is an internal bug fix; no application code, API, or configuration changes are required. Applications that previously worked around the crash (e.g. choosing `track $index` only to avoid the error) can re-verify array tracking behavior after upgrading.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// packages/forms/signals/src/field/node.ts (Angular 22.0.0, after fix)
markAsTouched(options?: MarkAsTouchedOptions): void {
  if (this.structure.isOrphaned()) {
    return;
  }
  untracked(() => {
    this.markAsTouchedInternal(options);
    this.flushSync();
  });
}
```
(https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/3c44d7c90b2392f7307d1b1dd0734db10ede63f5)

**What changed:** Angular 22 rewires how form resets propagate to custom (`ControlValueAccessor`-based) and Signal Form controls. In `@angular/forms/signals`, `FormFieldBindingOptions` gains an optional `readonly reset?: () => void`, `FormUiControl` gains an optional `reset?(): void`, and `FormField` gains a public `reset(): void` that invokes the registered resetter plus the parse-error reset callback (goldens/public-api/forms/signals/index.api.md). `FieldNode.reset()` now iterates `formFieldBindings()` and calls `binding.reset()`, so `control_cva.ts` unconditionally calls `writeValue` on reset (tracking previous written values in the bindings cache to avoid duplicate writes), and `control_native.ts` hooks `parent.onReset` to call `parser.reset()` and force `setNativeControlValue` DOM writes. `transformedValue` clears parser errors and the raw UI value on reset. The private token `ɵFORM_FIELD_PARSE_ERRORS` was replaced framework-wide by `ɵFORM_CONTROL_INTEGRATION` / `ɵFormControlIntegration` (with `setParseErrors` and `onReset`); the legacy forms provider `NG_CONTROL_PARSE_ERRORS_PROVIDER` was renamed to `NG_CONTROL_INTEGRATION_PROVIDER`, and `NgControl` now subscribes lazily to `control.events` (`FormResetEvent`) via `set onReset(callback)`. `Parser.reset()` also became a required interface member (internal). (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/849dba6c6506c2696a43a3fad6ee459e17b4b6c8)

**Affects:** Applications using Signal Forms custom controls (`FormValueControl` bound via `[formField]`), custom CVAs under `field()`, `transformedValue`, or code referencing the private symbols `ɵFORM_FIELD_PARSE_ERRORS` / `NG_CONTROL_PARSE_ERRORS_PROVIDER`. Behavior of `reset()` on forms/fields now visibly clears custom controls and parser validation errors.

**Action:** required: re-verify that custom controls reset correctly — implement `reset()` on `FormValueControl` controls or pass `reset` via `registerAsBinding({reset: ...})`/`FormFieldBindingOptions`; remove any references to `ɵFORM_FIELD_PARSE_ERRORS` or `NG_CONTROL_PARSE_ERRORS_PROVIDER` (renamed to `ɵFORM_CONTROL_INTEGRATION` / `NG_CONTROL_INTEGRATION_PROVIDER`); re-test `FormControl.reset()` / `FormGroup.reset()` on template-driven and reactive forms for new `writeValue` calls on CVAs.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// FormFieldBindingOptions (Signal Forms) — new optional member in v22
export interface FormFieldBindingOptions {
    readonly focus?: (focusOptions?: FocusOptions) => void;
    readonly reset?: () => void;          // added
}

// private DI token rename (legacy + signals bridge)
- ɵFORM_FIELD_PARSE_ERRORS   // removed
+ ɵFORM_CONTROL_INTEGRATION  // added (NG_CONTROL_INTEGRATION_PROVIDER)
```

**What changed:** In Signal Forms (`@angular/forms/signals`), two race conditions where a field node was removed from the model ("orphaned") while async work was in flight were fixed (commit 5835a5e3a73 in `packages/forms/signals`). (1) `FieldNode.debounceSync()` (`packages/forms/signals/src/field/node.ts`) now short-circuits with an early `return` when `this.structure.isOrphaned()` is true, immediately before calling `this.sync()`, preventing unhandled promise rejections from reading dead state after a `debounce(...)` promise resolves on a removed field. (2) `FieldValidationState.shouldSkipValidation` computed signal (`packages/forms/signals/src/field/validation.ts`) now includes `this.node.structure.isOrphaned()` alongside the existing `this.node.hidden() || this.node.disabled() || this.node.readonly()` checks, so the whole validation layer — sync and async errors, including in-flight `validateAsync` resolutions — is skipped for orphaned nodes instead of throwing/crashing. Reproduction specs were added to `orphan_repro.spec.ts` covering `debounceSync` and `validateAsync` (with `applyEach`, `debounce`, `validateAsync`, `resource`) race conditions (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/5835a5e3a73686473ad064f53f93d9d9acb541a6.patch).

**Affects:** Applications using signal forms (`form()`, `FormField`) with `debounce()` or `validateAsync()` on fields inside dynamically added/removed structures (e.g. array items removed while a debounce or async validation is pending).

**Action:** none — internal bug fix; no application code, API, or configuration changes required. Applications that previously crashed (orphan field errors / unhandled rejections) on removing fields mid-debounce or mid-async-validation now silently skip validation on the removed node; re-verify that behaviour if such crashes were being worked around.

**Before/after:**
```typescript
// before (validation.ts)
readonly shouldSkipValidation = computed(
  () => this.node.hidden() || this.node.disabled() || this.node.readonly(),
);

// after
readonly shouldSkipValidation = computed(
  () =>
    this.node.hidden() ||
    this.node.disabled() ||
    this.node.readonly() ||
    this.node.structure.isOrphaned(),
);

// node.ts — debounceSync(): now returns early when orphaned, before this.sync()
if (this.structure.isOrphaned()) {
  return;
}
