**What changed:** Angular 22's compiler now throws when two inputs/outputs bind to the same public name — e.g. `foo = model()` already emits a `fooChange` output, so declaring `fooChange = output()` (or `@Output() fooChange`) is a duplicate-output error (fix(compiler): throw on duplicate input/outputs, PR #65952, breaking change) (https://github.com/angular/angular/pull/65952). To fix affected code automatically, a new `ng update` migration `model-output` (version `22.0.0`, description "Migrate broken duplicate outputs", factory `./bundles/model-output.cjs#migrate`, in `packages/core/schematics/migrations.json`) rewrites such a `model()` into an `input()` + `linkedSignal()` pair: `foo = model(...)` becomes `fooInput = input(..., {alias: 'foo'}); foo = linkedSignal(this.fooInput);`, keeping the conflicting `fooChange = output()` (or `@Output()`) untouched, and adding `input` and `linkedSignal` to the `@angular/core` import. `model.required<T>()` becomes `input.required<T>({alias: 'foo'})`; existing model options (e.g. `debugName: 'my-foo'`) are merged into the input options unless an `alias` is already set; modifiers (`public`) are preserved; `@Output()`/`EventEmitter` conflicts are handled too (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/8f8972b0fdea2020800e7df5c6d85938602cb7e7, ).

**Affects:** Components/directives that declare both a `model()` signal and a separate property named `<model>Change` (as `output()` or `@Output()`); `model()` signals without a conflicting output are NOT migrated.

**Action:** required: run `ng update` (the automatic `model-output` migration rewrites these members). Since the model is replaced by an `input()` + `linkedSignal()`, re-verify any code that called the model as a writable signal setter (e.g. `foo.set(...)`/`foo.update(...)`); the migrated `foo` is now a read-only `linkedSignal` and the input `fooInput` is the write source. Verify the aliased template binding name `foo` and the `(fooChange)` output still behave as before.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// Before
foo = model(0);
fooChange = output<number>();

// After (produced by the model-output migration)
fooInput = input(0, {alias: 'foo'});
foo = linkedSignal(this.fooInput);
fooChange = output<number>();
```

**What changed:** This is an internal bug fix to the Angular schematic migration `packages/core/schematics/migrations/change-detection-eager/migration.ts` (`ChangeDetectionEagerMigration`), the migration that adds `changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager` to `@Component` metadata. Previously the migration inserted the new property **after the last existing property** in the `@Component({...})` object literal, using brittle string lookups to detect a trailing comma (TypeScript’s AST does not reflect commas). It now inserts the property **before the last pre-existing property**, computing indentation with `getLeadingLineWhitespaceOfNode(lastProp)` from `../../utils/tsurge/helpers/ast/leading_space`, which avoids generating syntactically invalid code (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/f01901d7668ab926bd7a786f43dbb18f2bb8a5b7). The updated test expectation shows the new placement: `template: '',\n  changeDetection: ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager,\n  standalone: true` (https://github.com/angular/angular/commit/f01901d7668ab926bd7a786f43dbb18f2bb8a5b7).

**Affects:** Only the generated output of the automatic `ChangeDetectionStrategy.Eager` migration; the property ordering inside the migrated `@Component` decorator may differ from earlier runs. No application code, template, or configuration change is required by the developer.

**Action:** none — re-verify only that the migration output compiles; the fix is fully internal to the schematic.

**Before/after:**
```ts
// migration.ts — before: insert after last property with comma string lookup
insertPos = lastProp.getEnd();
const textAfter = sf.text.substring(lastProp.getEnd());
const hasComma = /^\s*,/.test(textAfter);
const prefix = hasComma ? '' : ',';
toInsert = `${prefix}\n  changeDetection: ${exprText}.Eager`;

// after: insert before the last pre-existing property
insertPos = lastProp.getStart();
toInsert = `changeDetection: ${exprText}.Eager,\n${getLeadingLineWhitespaceOfNode(lastProp)}`;
```

